window.MWT_DOCS_BUNDLE = {"MWTP_50C_Split_Power_Design.md":"# MWTP-50C (\"KitchenShield-50C\") — Split-Power Restaurant Water Treatment System\r\n\r\n**Document ID:** `ENG-MWTP50C-01` — **Revision C (November 2026)**  \r\n**System:** Two trailers that work together:\r\n* **`MWTP-50C` Water Trailer** — 20-ft tandem-axle compact gooseneck, **no onboard generator**, 35 GPM continuous / 50 GPM peak at 60–75 PSI, runs on a **240V 50A shore-power cord**.\r\n* **`PWR-25` Generator & Outage-Kit Trailer** — towable 25 kVA diesel generator with its own fuel, a 50A output panel, and the outage kit (folding break tank and tanker suction hoses). It goes out **only on power-outage or zero-pressure jobs**.\r\n\r\n**Target Customers:** High-volume full-service restaurants, hotel kitchens, multi-unit restaurant groups, institutional kitchens (nursing homes, assisted living, schools, correctional facilities), and food processors — including existing AmeriPro F.O.G. and hood-cleaning accounts.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Why Split the Power Off the Water Trailer?\r\n\r\nA restaurant kitchen needs city power to cook. When its power is out, the kitchen is closed no matter what water it has — so on almost every restaurant job the building already has power, and an onboard generator would sit unused. Moving the generator to its own trailer, and making two Rev C process changes, gives:\r\n\r\n1. **A lighter, simpler water trailer.** No diesel, no fuel tank, no generator compartment, no transfer switch. On-site weight drops from 9,820 lb (MWTP-50) to **8,660 lb**, a **1,330 lb margin** under the 9,990 lb GVWR.\r\n2. **No ground tanks on Boil Water Advisory jobs.** Pressurized supply feeds the plant directly through an RPZ backflow preventer, and **virus-rated UV** (186 mJ/cm²) replaces the 600-gal chlorine contact tank. The trailer plugs in, connects two hoses, and runs — about **1.5 hours** from arrival to building water.\r\n3. **A generator AmeriPro controls.** Rental generators are hardest to find right after hurricanes and ice storms. An owned `PWR-25` is always available, and **one generator trailer can back up two or three water trailers**, because most restaurant jobs use the restaurant's own power.\r\n4. **A healthier generator.** Between emergencies the `PWR-25` can work on other AmeriPro jobs or run monthly against a load bank, so it starts when it's needed.\r\n5. **No CDL on either trailer.** Both are rated under 10,000 lb and each is towed by a standard 1-ton pickup.\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> **Approvals:** UV virus credit at a validated 186 mJ/cm² follows the EPA UV Disinfection Guidance Manual and the adenovirus dose table in the LT2 rule. The state drinking water program (Georgia EPD in our home state) confirms acceptance of UV as the virus barrier, and of RPZ direct feed in place of an air-gap break tank, during the pre-application meeting. Fabrication follows PE-stamped drawings.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. What Changed from the MWTP-50 (Rev B)\r\n\r\n| Item | `MWTP-50` (Rev B) | `MWTP-50C` (Rev C, Split Power) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Trailer** | 22-ft gooseneck (16 + 6 ft) | **20-ft gooseneck (14 + 6 ft)** |\r\n| **Power** | Onboard 20 kW generator + 50A shore inlet + ATS | **50A shore inlet only**; `PWR-25` generator trailer on outage jobs |\r\n| **Raw water supply (BWA)** | Air-gap fill into 550-gal ground break tank | **Direct feed through `PSV-100` + 1.5\" RPZ (`RPZ-100`)** |\r\n| **Raw water supply (outage)** | Tanker into ground break tank | **Draft from the tanker**, or the folding break tank carried on `PWR-25` |\r\n| **Virus barrier** | Chlorine contact time in 600-gal baffled ground tank | **UV validated at 186 mJ/cm²** (duty + standby) |\r\n| **Chlorine** | CT disinfection + residual | **Residual only** (1.0–2.0 mg/L) |\r\n| **Ground tanks on BWA jobs** | Two (break + contact) | **None** |\r\n| **Gooseneck deck** | Generator, fuel, air | **PLC panel, duplex air, segregated chemical cabinets** (exterior-access, vented) |\r\n| **Road / on-site weight** | 8,670 / 9,820 lb | **7,310 / 8,660 lb** |\r\n| **Arrival to building water** | ~2 hours | **~1.5 hours** (BWA); ~2 hours with generator |\r\n| **Turnkey cost** | $238,625 | **$219,420** water trailer + **$34,500** `PWR-25` = **$253,920** |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Process Design\r\n\r\n### 3.1 Treatment Train\r\n\r\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph IN[\"Stage 1: Direct Pressurized Supply (Port A) or Outage Draft (Port B)\"]\n        direction LR\n        TEE[\"Service-Line Tee or Hydrant<br/>(utility meter + backflow)\"] --> PSV[\"PT-100 + PSV-100<br/>(holds ≥ 20 PSI upstream)\"]\n        PSV --> RPZ1[\"RPZ-100: 1.5'' ASSE 1013<br/>Containment Backflow\"]\n        TANK[\"Outage: Tanker or PWR-25<br/>550-Gal Folding Break Tank\"] --> PORTB[\"Port B: 2'' Suction<br/>(XV-103 Valve)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph PRE[\"Stage 2: Boost/Draft Pump, Auto-Strainer & Bag/Carbon Pre-Filtration\"]\n        direction LR\n        XV100[\"XV-100 Port A<br/>Inlet Valve\"] --> P101[\"P-101: 3 HP VFD Pump<br/>(boost or suction draft)\"]\n        P101 --> STR[\"STR-101: 200 μm<br/>Auto-Strainer\"]\n        STR --> BF[\"BF-101A/B: Duplex 25 μm<br/>Bags / Carbon Block\"]\n        BF --> PAC[\"P-501 PACl + SM-101<br/>(only above 5 NTU)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph UF_UV[\"Stage 3: 0.02 μm Ultrafiltration & Stage 4: 186 mJ/cm² Virus-Rated UV\"]\n        direction LR\n        UFM[\"UF-201A/B: 4 × 60'' PVDF<br/>Modules + Daily DIT-201\"] --> AIT201[\"AIT-201 Permeate<br/>Turbidity (< 0.05 NTU)\"]\n        AIT201 --> UV[\"UV-401A/B: 186 mJ/cm² Validated<br/>(1 Duty + 1 Standby) + AIT-401 UVT\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph OUT[\"Stage 5 & 6: Chlorine Residual, 150-Gal Clearwell & 50 GPM Booster\"]\n        direction LR\n        CL[\"P-502A/B: NaOCl<br/>Residual Dosing\"] --> CW[\"TK-601: 150-Gal<br/>Onboard Clearwell\"]\n        CW --> BST[\"P-601A/B: Duplex 3 HP<br/>VFD Booster (50 GPM)\"]\n        BST --> RPZ2[\"RPZ-602: 2''<br/>ASSE 1013\"]\n        RPZ2 --> BLDG[\"Restaurant Domestic<br/>Tie-In (60–75 PSI)\"]\n    end\n\n    RPZ1 --> XV100\n    PORTB --> P101\n    PAC --> UFM\n    UV --> CL\n```\r\n\r\n### 3.2 Disinfection Credit Basis\r\n\r\n| Target | Barrier | How Credit Is Earned & Proven |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Cryptosporidium / Giardia** | UF + UV | UF credit from the modules' NSF/ANSI 419 challenge test, proven by a daily pressure-decay integrity test (`DIT-201`). UV at 186 mJ/cm² adds well over 4-log credit for both. |\r\n| **Bacteria** | UF + UV + chlorine residual | Physical removal plus UV inactivation; free chlorine residual protects the building piping. |\r\n| **Viruses** | **UV at a validated 186 mJ/cm²** | EPA's LT2 UV dose table credits **4-log virus** (based on adenovirus, the most UV-resistant) at 186 mJ/cm². Reactors are third-party validated under the UVDGM for this dose at the design flow and UVT. The PLC calculates delivered dose live from lamp intensity, UVT and flow. |\r\n\r\n**Why chlorine is residual-only:** with UV carrying the virus credit, chlorine's job is to keep the building's plumbing protected. No contact tank is needed. Target free chlorine is 1.5–2.0 mg/L leaving the clearwell and at least 1.0 mg/L at the farthest tap.\r\n\r\n### 3.3 UV Sizing (`UV-401A/B`)\r\n* **Dose:** ≥ 186 mJ/cm² validated reduction-equivalent dose at **40 GPM** and **85% UVT** (municipal water during a Boil Water Advisory typically runs 90–98% UVT).\r\n* **Configuration:** two identical reactors, **one duty + one 100% standby**, each able to carry full flow alone.\r\n* **Power:** about 1.1 kW for the duty reactor (low-pressure high-output amalgam lamps).\r\n* **Monitoring:** UV intensity sensor per reactor, online UVT analyzer (`AIT-401`), and flow from `FIT-200`.\r\n\r\n### 3.4 Direct Feed Through an RPZ (`RPZ-100`)\r\n* The service-line tee (installed by the restaurant's plumber at the site survey) or the hydrant (through the utility's meter and backflow device) feeds **Port A**.\r\n* **`PSV-100`** holds the upstream line at **20 PSI or more**, so the plant can never pull the city main or the restaurant's service down.\r\n* **`RPZ-100`** (1.5\" ASSE 1013) is the trailer's own containment backflow protection — it replaces the air gap that the ground break tank provided. Its relief port drains through the floor with a flow switch (`FS-100`) that alarms on discharge.\r\n* **`P-101`** runs in **boost mode**: with 40–70 PSI city pressure it only adds what the UF feed needs, so it draws less power than in Rev B. A suction pressure switch (`PSL-101`) downstream of the RPZ protects the pump.\r\n* Building isolation stays **double-block**: two closed building valves (or one valve leak-tested closed on each deployment) between the supply tee and the treated-water tie-in.\r\n\r\n### 3.5 Outage Mode (Zero Main Pressure)\r\n* **Port B** (2\" suction, `XV-103`) lets `P-101` **draft straight from a potable tanker**, so the tanker itself is the break tank.\r\n* When a tanker can't stay on site, the **550-gal folding break tank carried on `PWR-25`** is set up and filled by tanker drops. Port A stays closed and capped.\r\n* A typical restaurant uses 4,000–8,000 gal/day, so one 4,000-gal tanker drop covers most of a service day.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Valve Register (17 Automated Valves)\r\n\r\n| Tag | Size | Service | Normal | Fail Position |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| `XV-100` | 1.5\" | Port A pressurized inlet (after `RPZ-100`) | Open (BWA) | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-103` | 2.0\" | Port B outage suction (tanker / folding tank) | Closed (BWA) | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-101` | 1.0\" | Strainer blowdown | Pulsing | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-201A/B` | 1.5\" | UF sub-train A/B feed | Open | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-202A/B` | 1.5\" | UF sub-train A/B permeate | Open | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-204A/B` | 1.5\" | UF sub-train A/B top backwash drain | Closed | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-205A/B` | 1.5\" | UF sub-train A/B bottom drain | Closed | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-203` | 1.5\" | Shared backwash supply (from clearwell via `P-601B`) | Closed | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-206` | 0.5\" | Shared air scour | Closed | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-207` | 0.5\" | Shared integrity-test air | Closed | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-300` | 1.5\" | Off-spec permeate divert to waste | Closed | **Fail-Open** |\r\n| `XV-601` | 2.0\" | Clearwell drain | Closed | Fail-Closed |\r\n| `XV-602` | 2.0\" | Potable outlet isolation | Open | Fail-Closed |\r\n\r\nDuring a backwash or integrity test, the sub-train under test is isolated by its own feed and permeate valves while the shared backwash, air scour or test-air valve opens. The other sub-train keeps running at about 25 GPM.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Interlocks & Alarms\r\n\r\n| Tag | Measurement | Trip Point | Automatic Action |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `PT-100` | Upstream supply pressure | < 22 PSI alarm / < 20 PSI low-low | `PSV-100` holds 20 PSI; on low-low `XV-100` closes and the HMI prompts **\"SWITCH TO PORT B — TANKER / OUTAGE KIT.\"** |\r\n| `PSL-101` | Pump suction (after `RPZ-100`) | < 10 PSI (boost) / loss of prime (draft) | Slows, then trips `P-101`. |\r\n| `FS-100` | `RPZ-100` relief discharge | Sustained flow | Alarm — check RPZ; continue only if relief stops. |\r\n| `PDIT-201` | UF TMP / daily pressure decay | TMP > 22 PSI / decay above limit | Backwash / hold sub-train offline; plant runs ~25 GPM on the passing sub-train. |\r\n| `AIT-201` | UF permeate turbidity | > 0.15 NTU for 5 s | Opens `XV-300` to waste, closes `XV-602`. |\r\n| `UIT-401A/B`, `AIT-401` | Calculated UV dose (intensity, UVT, flow) | < 186 mJ/cm² | Starts standby reactor; if still low, cuts flow to the validated limit; if still low, **closes `XV-602`** (virus credit lost). |\r\n| `AIT-601` | Free chlorine & pH (clearwell outlet) | < 0.5 or > 4.0 mg/L | Switches to standby pump `P-502B`; closes `XV-602` if not corrected in 30 s. |\r\n| `LIT-601` | Clearwell level | Low-low < 20% | Trips boosters and backwash. |\r\n| `PT-603` | Building pressure (remote) | > 90 PSI | Trips lead booster. |\r\n| `CT-SHORE` | Shore-power current | > 38.5 A | Sheds HVAC compressor until load drops. |\r\n| `AIT-901` | Chemical cabinet Cl₂ gas | ≥ 0.5 ppm | Exhaust fan to high, alarm, stop chemical transfers. |\r\n| `PWR-LOSS` | Supply voltage | Loss of shore or `PWR-25` power | All valves fail safe (`XV-602` closed, `XV-300` open); PLC on UPS sends a telemetry alarm to the on-call operator. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Water Trailer Layout (`MWTP-50C`: 14-ft Main Deck + 6-ft Gooseneck Deck)\r\n\r\n* **Main box:** 14 ft × 8.0 ft exterior (7.5 ft inside) × 7.5 ft interior height, about **10 ft 3 in** overall road height.\r\n* **Gooseneck deck (exterior access):** PLC panel `CP-701` behind a weatherproof side door, duplex air compressors and receiver, and the **segregated chemical cabinets** (oxidizer separate from acid/reducer) with their own exhaust fan and `AIT-901`. Keeping chemicals in the nose — outside the operator space — also puts weight over the hitch.\r\n* **Main deck:** inlet skid and UF up front, clearwell and boosters over the axles, UV and the rear camlock bulkhead at the back, with a fold-down test bench and eyewash beside the side door.\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TB\r\n    subgraph GN[\"Gooseneck Deck (0-6 ft, exterior-access doors)\"]\r\n        direction LR\r\n        PLC[\"CP-701 PLC / HMI Panel + UPS + Telemetry\"]\r\n        AIR[\"Duplex Oil-Less Air + Receiver\"]\r\n        CHEM[\"Segregated Chem Cabinets (NaOCl vs. Citric / Bisulfite) + Exhaust + AIT-901\"]\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    subgraph MAIN[\"14-ft Main Treatment Deck (6-20 ft, 30-inch walkway)\"]\r\n        direction TB\r\n        subgraph FRONT[\"Front (6-11 ft)\"]\r\n            direction LR\r\n            INLET[\"Curbside: PSV-100, RPZ-100, P-101, STR-101, BF-101A/B\"]\r\n            UFZ[\"Roadside: UF-201A/B (4 x 60'' Modules) + DIT-201\"]\r\n        end\r\n        subgraph MID[\"Over Axles (11-16 ft)\"]\r\n            direction LR\r\n            CWZ[\"TK-601 150-Gal Clearwell + P-601A/B Booster\"]\r\n            BENCH[\"Fold-Down Test Bench, Analyzers & Eyewash\"]\r\n        end\r\n        subgraph REAR[\"Rear (16-20 ft)\"]\r\n            direction LR\r\n            UVZ[\"UV-401A/B (186 mJ/cm2) + NaOCl Residual Dosing\"]\r\n            BULK[\"RPZ-602 + Rear Bulkhead: Port A, Port B, Potable Out, Waste, 50A Shore Inlet\"]\r\n        end\r\n        FRONT --> MID --> REAR\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    GN ===|\"Weatherproof bulkhead\"| MAIN\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 6.1 Rear Bulkhead Ports\r\n1. **`PORT A` (1.5\" camlock, blue):** pressurized raw supply from the service-line tee or hydrant → `PT-100` → `PSV-100` → `RPZ-100`.\r\n2. **`PORT B` (2\" camlock, blue):** outage suction from a tanker or the folding break tank.\r\n3. **`POTABLE OUT` (2\" & 1.5\" camlock, white NSF-61):** pressurized kitchen supply after `RPZ-602` and `XV-602`.\r\n4. **`WASTE` (2\" camlock, gray):** dechlorinated backwash and flush water to the **downstream** sanitary sewer cleanout — never upstream of the grease interceptor.\r\n5. **`SHORE 50A`:** CS6375 / NEMA 14-50 twist-lock inlet for the restaurant receptacle or the `PWR-25` cord.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Weights (Both Trailers Under 10,000 lb — No CDL)\r\n\r\n### 7.1 `MWTP-50C` Water Trailer\r\n\r\n| Subsystem | Road (lbs) | On-Site (lbs) | Location |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| **20-ft aluminum gooseneck** (insulation, HVAC, jacks, exterior-access nose cabinet; dual 5,200 lb axles plated 9,990 lb GVWR; **certified tare ≤ 3,300 lb**) | 3,300 | 3,300 | Full frame |\r\n| **Gooseneck deck:** PLC panel, duplex air, segregated chemical cabinets + chemicals (materials-of-trade limits) | 870 | 870 | Gooseneck (0–6 ft) |\r\n| **Inlet skid:** `PSV-100`, `RPZ-100`, `P-101`, `STR-101`, `BF-101A/B` | 450 | 520 | Front curbside |\r\n| **UF skid:** 4 × 60\" modules (preserved wet in transit) + `DIT-201` | 890 | 1,020 | Front roadside |\r\n| **UV-401A/B** (186 mJ/cm²) + residual dosing | 360 | 420 | Rear |\r\n| **Clearwell (150 gal, 85% level on site) + duplex booster + `RPZ-602`** | 590 | 1,650 | Over axles |\r\n| **Piping, 17 valves, hoses & 50 ft 50A cord reel** | 600 | 600 | Distributed |\r\n| **Fold-down bench, analyzers & eyewash** | 250 | 280 | Over axles |\r\n| **TOTAL** | **7,310 lbs** | **8,660 lbs** | **9,990 lb GVWR** |\r\n\r\n* **Margins:** 2,680 lb (27%) on the road; 1,330 lb on site.\r\n* **Pin weight:** with the control panel, air and chemical cabinets in the nose, about **1,170 lb (16%)** on the road — inside the 15–25% range a gooseneck needs. Confirmed on a certified scale after fit-out.\r\n\r\n### 7.2 `PWR-25` Generator & Outage-Kit Trailer\r\n\r\n| Item | Weight (lbs) |\r\n| :--- | :---: |\r\n| Towable 25 kVA diesel generator on factory trailer (sound-attenuated, integrated fuel tank, 110% spill containment base), fuel full | ~3,100 |\r\n| 50A distribution panel, 2 × 50 ft #6/4 SOOW cords, ground rod kit | 120 |\r\n| Outage kit box: 550-gal folding break tank, 2\" suction hoses, tanker adapters | 260 |\r\n| Fuel transfer pump & spill kit | 60 |\r\n| **TOTAL** | **~3,540 lbs** (under 10,000 lb — no CDL) |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 8. Electrical Design (240V Single-Phase, Shore Cord Only)\r\n\r\n| Load | Rating | Running kW (35 GPM) | Peak kW |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| `P-101` inlet pump (VFD, boost mode) | 3 HP | 1.20 | 2.20 |\r\n| `P-601A/B` duplex booster (VFD) | 2 × 3 HP | 2.10 | 4.40 |\r\n| `UV-401A` duty reactor (186 mJ/cm²) | ~1.1 kW | 1.10 | 1.10 |\r\n| Duplex air compressors | 1.5 HP | 0.35 | 1.20 |\r\n| PLC, analyzers, dosing pumps, lighting | 120V | 0.50 | 0.65 |\r\n| HVAC heat pump / freeze heater | 12,000 BTU | 2.20 | 2.80 |\r\n| **TOTAL** | — | **7.45 kW (~31 A @ 240V)** | **12.35 kW (~51 A)** |\r\n\r\n* **On restaurant shore power (BWA jobs):** a 50A receptacle carries 40A continuous (9.6 kW). The PLC sheds the HVAC compressor during booster peaks and backwashes, which keeps the peak at **9.55 kW**. The restaurant's electricity cost is roughly 180 kWh/day.\r\n* **On `PWR-25` (outage jobs):** a 25 kVA generator runs at about 37% average load. Fuel burn is roughly **0.8 gal/hr (~19 gal/day)**, so its integrated tank covers more than two days between refuelings.\r\n* **Grounding:** on shore power the neutral-to-ground bond is at the restaurant's service panel. On `PWR-25` the generator is a separately derived system with its neutral bonded at the generator and a ground rod at the generator trailer; the water trailer's panel keeps neutral and ground separate in both cases.\r\n* **Site survey item:** each covered location gets a **240V 50A receptacle** installed by the restaurant's electrician (typically $800–$2,000), included in the survey scope.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 9. Bill of Materials & Turnkey Cost\r\n\r\n### 9.1 `MWTP-50C` Water Trailer\r\n\r\n| Category | Description | Cost (USD) | % of Hardware |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **1.0** | 20-ft aluminum gooseneck (14 + 6 ft), insulation, HVAC, jacks, exterior-access nose cabinet | $28,500 | 18.3% |\r\n| **2.0** | Inlet: `PSV-100` kit, 1.5\" `RPZ-100` + relief flow switch, 3 HP `P-101`, auto-strainer, duplex bag/carbon housings | $14,400 | 9.2% |\r\n| **3.0** | UF skid: 4 × 60\" PVDF modules (2,200 ft²) + automated `DIT-201` | $26,500 | 17.0% |\r\n| **4.0** | UV-401A/B validated at 186 mJ/cm² (duty + standby), UVT analyzer, residual dosing pumps, segregated chemical cabinets | $30,200 | 19.4% |\r\n| **5.0** | 150-gal clearwell, duplex 3 HP VFD booster, 2\" `RPZ-602` | $14,800 | 9.5% |\r\n| **6.0** | 316L piping, 17 actuated valves, rear bulkhead, sanitized hose kit | $15,500 | 9.9% |\r\n| **7.0** | PLC/HMI with UPS & telemetry, turbidimeter, chlorine/pH analyzer, Cl₂ gas sensor, eyewash | $19,900 | 12.8% |\r\n| **8.0** | 50A shore inlet with surge protection & cord reel; duplex oil-less air | $6,000 | 3.9% |\r\n| — | **SUBTOTAL — MAJOR EQUIPMENT** | **$155,800** | **100.0%** |\r\n| **9.0** | Integration, TIG welding, panel wiring, factory acceptance test | $22,000 | — |\r\n| **10.0** | Engineering: PE basis of design, stamped P&IDs, UV validation & state submittal | $13,000 | — |\r\n| **11.0** | Contingency (15% of 1.0–10.0) | $28,620 | — |\r\n| — | **WATER TRAILER TURNKEY** | **$219,420** | — |\r\n\r\n### 9.2 `PWR-25` Generator & Outage-Kit Trailer\r\n\r\n| Item | Description | Cost (USD) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :---: |\r\n| **G1** | Towable 25 kVA diesel generator, Tier 4 Final, sound-attenuated, 240V single-phase / 3-phase selectable, integrated fuel tank & spill base | $27,500 |\r\n| **G2** | 50A output panel with GFCI, 2 × 50 ft #6/4 SOOW cords, ground rod kit | $1,800 |\r\n| **G3** | 550-gal folding NSF-61 break tank (outage kit) | $2,400 |\r\n| **G4** | 2\" suction hoses, tanker adapters & camlocks | $1,200 |\r\n| **G5** | Lockable kit box | $900 |\r\n| **G6** | Fuel transfer pump & spill kit | $700 |\r\n| — | **`PWR-25` TOTAL** | **$34,500** |\r\n\r\n### 9.3 System Cost Comparison\r\n\r\n| Configuration | One Water Unit | Two Water Units |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **`MWTP-50` (Rev B, generator on board)** | $238,625 | $477,250 |\r\n| **`MWTP-50C` + one shared `PWR-25`** | **$253,920** | **$473,340** |\r\n\r\nFor a single unit, Rev C costs about $15,000 more. From the second unit on, it costs less, because one `PWR-25` backs up the whole restaurant fleet.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 10. Field Operation\r\n\r\n### 10.1 Boil Water Advisory Job (Restaurant Has Power) — about 1.5 Hours\r\n1. **Position & level** the water trailer behind the kitchen (2 parking spaces), set the jacks, and plug the 50A cord into the restaurant's receptacle.\r\n2. **Isolate the building:** utility or facility closes both building valves (double isolation) and tags them.\r\n3. **Connect Port A** to the pre-installed service-line tee, the **waste hose** to the downstream sewer cleanout, and the **sanitized potable hose** to the domestic tie-in.\r\n4. **Start-up:** flush to waste until permeate is below 0.05 NTU, run the UF integrity test, and confirm UV dose ≥ 186 mJ/cm² and chlorine residual 1.5–2.0 mg/L.\r\n5. **Pressurize** the building at 30 PSI, bleed air at the farthest fixture, then raise to 60–75 PSI. Pull Bac-T samples per the state's emergency approval.\r\n\r\n### 10.2 Power-Outage or Zero-Pressure Job — about 2 Hours\r\n1. A second pickup brings the **`PWR-25`**. Park it at least 10 ft from building openings and the dining area, downwind where possible; drive the ground rod; run the 50A cord to the water trailer's shore inlet.\r\n2. **Zero main pressure:** set up the folding break tank from the `PWR-25` kit box (or park the tanker beside the trailer) and connect it to **Port B**. Port A stays capped.\r\n3. Continue with steps 2–5 of §10.1.\r\n\r\n### 10.3 Staffing\r\nA state-certified operator attends through the kitchen's service hours (about 12 hours). Overnight the unit runs on cellular telemetry with automatic outlet lockout and a one-hour on-call response, subject to the state's emergency-use approval. On outage jobs, the `PWR-25` driver delivers and leaves; the operator handles refueling checks.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 11. Unit Economics (One Water Trailer + One `PWR-25`)\r\n\r\n**Pricing** is the same as the `MWTP-50`: $1,800/yr single-location retainer or $1,500/yr per store for groups (**max 10 covered locations per water trailer**), $2,500 mobilization, $3,200 per 24-hour day. Outage jobs add a **generator add-on: $750 delivery + $400/day**.\r\n\r\n### 11.1 Financing\r\n\r\n| Option | Project Cost | 15% Equity | 85% Loan (8.75%, 10 yr) | Annual Debt Service |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **A — Existing AmeriPro pickups** (water trailer + `PWR-25` + $21,375 spares & working capital) | $275,295 | $41,294 | $234,001 | **$35,196** ($2,933/mo) |\r\n| **B — New 1-ton pickup** (+ $62,000 truck, $24,375 spares & working capital) | $340,295 | $51,044 | $289,251 | **$43,500** ($3,625/mo) |\r\n\r\n### 11.2 Year 1 (8 Covered Locations, 6 Events / 25 Active Days, 2 Outage Events)\r\n\r\n| Line Item | Year 1 |\r\n| :--- | :---: |\r\n| Retainers (3 single + 5 group stores) | $12,900 |\r\n| Mobilization (6 events × $2,500) | $15,000 |\r\n| Treatment days (25 × $3,200) | $80,000 |\r\n| Generator add-on (2 outage events × [$750 + 3 days × $400]) | $3,900 |\r\n| **Gross revenue** | **$111,800** |\r\n| Direct costs: labor $580/day, chemicals & Bac-T $89/day, diesel on 6 generator days, $450/mobilization, $300 per generator delivery | ($20,481) |\r\n| Fixed overhead ($28,000 water trailer + $2,500 `PWR-25` insurance, registration & service) | ($30,500) |\r\n| **EBITDA** | **$60,819** |\r\n| **DSCR — Option A** (after $25k owner draw) | **1.73×** *(1.02×)* |\r\n| **DSCR — Option B** (after $25k owner draw) | **1.40×** *(0.82×)* |\r\n\r\nYear 1 coverage is about the same as the `MWTP-50` (1.76× / 1.41×). The advantages of Rev C show up in operations — lighter trailer, faster setup, no ground tanks on most jobs, a generator that is always available — and in the lower cost of each additional water trailer.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 12. Recommendation\r\n\r\nBuild the `MWTP-50C` + `PWR-25` if AmeriPro plans to grow past one restaurant unit, or can use the generator on other work between emergencies. For a single unit that will rarely see outage jobs, the `MWTP-50` (Rev B) and `MWTP-50C` cost about the same; the `MWTP-50C`'s lighter, simpler water trailer and faster setup make it the better field unit. The hospital `MWTP-250` keeps its onboard 80 kW generator and its contact tank: hospital jobs are where power and water fail together, and virus-rated UV at 250 GPM is large and costly.\r\n","MWTP_50_Restaurant_Compact_Unit_Design.md":"# MWTP-50 (\"KitchenShield-50\") — Compact Restaurant Mobile Water Treatment Unit\r\n\r\n**Document ID:** `ENG-MWTP50-REST-01` — **Revision B (November 2026)**  \r\n**Target Customer Base:** Commercial Kitchens, Full-Service Restaurants, Fast-Casual Chains, Hotel Kitchens, and Existing AmeriPro F.O.G. (Fats, Oils & Grease) / Exhaust Hood Cleaning Accounts  \r\n**Rated Capacity:** **35 GPM Continuous Treatment (50,400 GPD)** | **50 GPM Peak Kitchen Booster Delivery (60–75 PSI)**  \r\n**Mobility Platform:** **22-ft Tandem-Axle Compact Gooseneck Trailer (`16-ft` Main Deck + `6-ft` Gooseneck Deck)** — **9,990 lb GVWR (No Class A CDL Required)**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Strategic & Operational Rationale\r\n\r\n### 1.1 Why a Dedicated 35–50 GPM Compact Gooseneck Unit for Restaurants?\r\nWhile the flagship **MWTP-250 (250 GPM, 36-ft gooseneck, $601k build cost)** is engineered for multi-story acute-care hospitals, a typical full-service restaurant or commercial kitchen uses **2,500 to 8,000 gallons per day** (an average of **4 to 12 GPM** during operating hours) with short **25 to 50 GPM peak bursts** when the commercial dish machine, 3-compartment prep sinks, wok stations, and restroom flushometers operate simultaneously.\r\n\r\nBuilding the **MWTP-50 (\"KitchenShield-50\")** first — or alongside the MWTP-250 — provides five decisive operational and commercial advantages for AmeriPro:\r\n\r\n1. **No Class A CDL Required (Under 10,000 lb Trailer GVWR & Under 26,001 lb GCWR):**\r\n   * Federal FMCSA regulations (`49 CFR § 383.91`) require a **Class A CDL** only when the combined Gross Combination Weight Rating (`GCWR`) is **26,001 lbs or more** inclusive of a towed unit with a `GVWR` of **more than 10,000 lbs**.\r\n   * By packaging the MWTP-50 on a **22-ft tandem-axle compact gooseneck trailer (`16-ft` main deck + `6-ft` gooseneck deck)** factory-plated at **9,990 lb GVWR** and towing it with a standard **3/4-ton or 1-ton single-rear-wheel (SRW) pickup truck** (`F-250/F-350` or `Ram 2500/3500`, `10,000–11,500 lb GVWR` with factory gooseneck ball), the combined `GCWR` is **19,990 to 21,490 lbs**.\r\n   * Federal rules count the truck's GCWR as the greater of the manufacturer's rating or the sum of the two GVWRs, and some 1-ton pickups carry a manufacturer GCWR above 26,000 lbs. That's still fine: a Class A CDL is only triggered when the towed unit is rated **over 10,000 lbs**, and this trailer is plated at 9,990 lbs.\r\n   * **Result:** Any trained AmeriPro service technician or water operator with a **standard driver's license** and a basic **DOT medical card** (required for commercial combinations over 10,001 lb GCWR) can legally tow and position the trailer — eliminating the Class A CDL driver bottleneck.\r\n2. **Tight Alley & Drive-Thru Maneuverability (Compact Gooseneck Advantage):**\r\n   * Because the gooseneck coupler pivots directly over the 1-ton pickup's rear axle, the truck can make **near-90° jackknife turns** to back the 16-ft main box into tight restaurant service alleys, grease-trap lanes, and drive-thru corridors where a bumper-pull trailer would bind or swing wide.\r\n   * Putting the **20 kW diesel generator, 40-gal fuel tank, and air compressors** on the **6-ft elevated gooseneck deck** behind a **1-hour fire and acoustic bulkhead wall** places **1,730 lbs (20%)** directly over the pickup's rear axle for sway-free highway handling and keeps engine noise and heat out of the treatment room.\r\n3. **Fits in 2–3 Restaurant Parking Spaces:**\r\n   * A 36-ft hospital trailer plus two 3,000-gallon pillow tanks takes up 4–6 parking stalls. The MWTP-50 and its compact 550-gal / 600-gal folding ground tanks fit into **two to three standard parking spaces** behind the kitchen next to the grease trap and backflow preventer.\r\n4. **Can Run on Restaurant 240V 50A Shore Power OR Its Own 20 kW Generator:**\r\n   * Because the MWTP-50's average electrical draw is only **8.25 kW (~34 Amps at 240V single-phase)**, during a **Boil Water Advisory** (when grid power is still on) the trailer can plug directly into a **240V 50A NEMA 14-50 / CS6365 receptacle** installed during the site survey — running silently with **zero diesel exhaust and zero fuel cost** right outside the dining patio. When grid power is out, the onboard **20 kW Tier-4 diesel generator** on the gooseneck deck carries the full load automatically.\r\n5. **Direct Cross-Sell to Existing AmeriPro Restaurant Accounts:**\r\n   * AmeriPro already services commercial kitchens for **F.O.G. (grease trap pumping)** and **exhaust hood cleaning**. The general manager, kitchen layout, back alley, sewer cleanout, and water shutoff are already known to AmeriPro's route teams.\r\n6. **60% Lower Capital Cost ($238,625 vs. $601,450):**\r\n   * Lower capital risk makes it easy to finance, faster to build, and an ideal platform to establish AmeriPro's **state drinking-water emergency-use approval** and operating track record.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Sizing Basis: Commercial Kitchen Hydraulic Profile\r\n\r\n| Kitchen Fixture / Sub-System | Typical Flow Rate | Duty Cycle | Why Untreated / Boil-Water Tap Fails |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| **High-Temp / Chemical Dish Machine** | 12–18 GPM (fill/rinse bursts) | Intermittent (every 90 sec) | Requires ≥ 20 PSI dynamic flow (60 PSI line pressure); solenoid screens clog from main-break sediment. |\r\n| **3-Compartment & Prep Sinks** | 8–15 GPM | Continuous during prep | Health code prohibits washing produce or dishes in unapproved water during a BWA. |\r\n| **Ice Machines, Soda Fountain & Espresso** | 2–5 GPM | Continuous make-up | Ice and fountain drinks are direct ingestion hazards; 0.02 μm UF + UV + Cl₂ protects carbon filters and ice plates. |\r\n| **Customer & Staff Restrooms** | 10–20 GPM (flushometer peaks) | Intermittent bursts | Tankless flushometer valves require **≥ 35–45 PSI at 15+ GPM** just to close properly. |\r\n| **Combined Restaurant Profile** | **8–20 GPM Average** / **35 GPM Continuous** / **50 GPM Peak (3-min burst)** | **4,000–12,000 Gal/Day** | **MWTP-50 treats 35 GPM continuously and buffers 50 GPM peak bursts from its onboard 150-gal clearwell.** |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Non-CDL Weight & Axle Balance (`MWTP-50-GN22`)\r\n\r\n> [!IMPORTANT]\r\n> **How the MWTP-50 Stays Under 10,000 lb GVWR Both on the Road and On-Site:**\r\n> The raw-water break tank (`TK-100`, 550 gal) and the baffled chlorine contact tank (`TK-401`, 600 gal) are **collapsible NSF-61 ground tanks deployed beside the trailer**, so 9,590 lbs of water rests on the pavement rather than on the trailer frame. On site, the onboard **150-gal clearwell (`TK-601`)** runs at its 85% control level (about 1,060 lbs of water), bringing the trailer to **9,820 lbs** — inside the **9,990 lb rating**. On the road the trailer weighs **8,670 lbs**, a 1,320 lb (13%) margin.\r\n>\r\n> **Builder spec:** the trailer's certified empty (tare) weight, including insulation, firewall, HVAC and jacks, must not exceed **3,600 lbs** (aluminum frame and skin). The builder's certified tare is checked before any equipment is ordered, and the rig is weighed on a certified scale after fit-out.\r\n\r\n| Subsystem / Component | Road Transport Weight (lbs) | On-Site Operating Weight (lbs) | Location in Trailer |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| **22-ft Aluminum Gooseneck Trailer (`16-ft` Main + `6-ft` Neck, Insulation, 1-Hr Firewall, HVAC & Jacks; Dual 5,200 lb Torsion Axles Plated at 9,990 lb GVWR)** | 3,600 | 3,600 | Full Frame + 4 Corners |\r\n| **Gooseneck Power & Air Deck: 20 kW Tier-4 Diesel Gen + 40-Gal Fuel + Duplex Air** | 1,450 | 1,450 | Gooseneck Deck (`0–6 ft`) |\r\n| **Stage 1 & 2: Raw Intake Pump (`P-101`), Auto-Strainer (`STR-101`) & Duplex Bag Filters (`BF-101A/B`)** | 380 | 450 | Front Curbside (`6–11 ft`) |\r\n| **Stage 3: Compact Hollow-Fiber UF Skid (4 × 60\" Modules, Preserved Wet in Transit) + `DIT-201`** | 890 | 1,020 | Mid Curbside (`11–16 ft`) |\r\n| **Stage 4 & 5: Duty/Standby 40 GPM UV (`UV-401A/B`), Segregated Chem Cabinets & Dosing Skid** | 420 | 470 | Mid Roadside (`11–16 ft`) |\r\n| **Stage 6: 150-Gal Onboard Clearwell (`TK-601`), Duplex 3 HP Booster (`P-601A/B`) & 2\" RPZ (`RPZ-602`)** | 590 | 1,650 | Over Axles (`12–17 ft`) |\r\n| **316L SS Schedule 10/40 Piping, 12 Actuated Valves (`XV`), Hoses & Stowed Ground Tanks** | 720 | 560 | Distributed / Rear Reels |\r\n| **Control Panel (`CP-701`), Analyzers (`AIT-201/401/402/601/901`), Fold-Down Test Bench & Eyewash** | 420 | 420 | Front Roadside (`6–11 ft`) |\r\n| **Chemicals (Materials-of-Trade Limits: 15 gal 12.5% NaOCl, 5 gal Citric, 5 gal Bisulfite)** | 200 | 200 | Rear Curbside (`17–21 ft`) |\r\n| **TOTAL ON-TRAILER WEIGHT** | **8,670 lbs** | **9,820 lbs** | **9,990 lb Max GVWR** |\r\n| *Gooseneck Coupler Pin Weight (20% Road)* | *1,730 lbs* | *Supported by 4 Jacks* | *1-Ton SRW Truck Bed Ball* |\r\n| *Tandem Axle Load (2 × 5,200 lb Axles = 10,400 lb Raw Capacity, Plated at 9,990 lb GVWR)* | *6,940 lbs* | *Supported by 4 Jacks* | *3,470 lbs per axle on road* |\r\n| **Ground-Deployed Tanks (`TK-100` 550 gal + `TK-401` 600 gal)** | *(Stowed above: 160 lbs)* | **9,590 lbs (On Ground)** | **Next to Trailer on Pavement** |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Six-Stage Treatment Process & Disinfection Credit Basis\r\n\r\nThe MWTP-50 applies the exact same **multi-barrier UF + UV + Free Chlorine CT** engineering and regulatory rigor as Revision B of the MWTP-250, scaled to **35 GPM continuous treatment** and **50 GPM peak delivery**.\r\n\r\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph S1[\"Stage 1: Raw Water Supply & Air-Gap Break Tank\"]\n        direction LR\n        SRC[\"Hydrant (≥ 20 PSI via PSV-100)<br/>OR Service-Line Tap OR Tanker\"] --> TK100[\"TK-100: 550-Gal Air-Gap<br/>Break Tank (Ground)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph S2[\"Stage 2: Intake, Auto-Strainer, Bag/Carbon & Coagulation\"]\n        direction LR\n        P101[\"P-101: 3 HP VFD<br/>Intake Pump (40 GPM)\"] --> STR[\"STR-101: 1.5'' Auto-<br/>Strainer (200 μm)\"]\n        STR --> BF[\"BF-101A/B: Duplex #2<br/>Bag / Carbon (25 μm)\"]\n        BF --> PAC[\"P-501: PACl Coagulant<br/>+ SM-101 Static Mixer\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph S3[\"Stage 3: 0.02 μm Hollow-Fiber Ultrafiltration & Stage 4: Redundant UV\"]\n        direction LR\n        UF[\"UF-201A/B: 4 × 60'' PVDF<br/>Modules + Daily DIT-201\"] --> AIT201[\"AIT-201: Laser<br/>Nephelometer (< 0.05 NTU)\"]\n        AIT201 --> UV[\"UV-401A/B: 2 × 40 GPM<br/>Reactors (1+1, ≥ 40 mJ/cm²)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph S5[\"Stage 5: 4-Log Virus Chlorine Contact Time (CT)\"]\n        direction LR\n        CL2[\"P-502A/B: 12.5%<br/>NaOCl Dosing\"] --> TK401[\"TK-401: 600-Gal Baffled<br/>Contact Tank (T10 = 8.5 min)\"]\n        TK401 --> P401[\"P-401: 1.5 HP CT<br/>Transfer Pump\"]\n        P401 --> AIT601[\"AIT-601: Free Cl2 & pH<br/>(1.5–2.2 mg/L)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph S6[\"Stage 6: 150-Gal Surge Clearwell & 50 GPM Peak Kitchen Booster\"]\n        direction LR\n        TK601[\"TK-601: 150-Gal<br/>Onboard Clearwell\"] --> P601[\"P-601A/B: Duplex 3 HP<br/>VFD Booster (50 GPM @ 60–75 PSI)\"]\n        P601 --> RPZ[\"RPZ-602: 2'' ASSE 1013<br/>Backflow Preventer\"]\n        RPZ --> KIT[\"Restaurant Domestic Tie-In<br/>(Upstream of Ice, Dish & Sinks)\"]\n    end\n\n    TK100 --> P101\n    PAC --> UF\n    UV --> CL2\n    AIT601 --> TK601\n```\r\n\r\n### Stage 1: Raw Water Supply (Three Restaurant Feed Options)\r\n1. **Option 1A — Boil Water Advisory via a Pre-Installed Service-Line Tap (No Hydrant Required):**\r\n   * During a Boil Water Advisory where the street main still has normal pressure (`40–70 PSI`), a restaurant only needs **15–35 GPM** (5,000–10,000 gal/day).\r\n   * During the site survey, the restaurant's licensed plumber installs a **1.5\" valved tee with a capped camlock** on the service line **between the meter and the building isolation valves**, with the water utility's approval. Hose bibbs and backflow test cocks are not used — test cocks can't pass 35 GPM, and most exterior bibbs sit downstream of the building shutoff.\r\n   * **Double isolation:** the tie-in design puts **two closed valves** between the raw supply tee and the treated-water tie-in (the building isolation valve plus a second valve, or one valve leak-tested closed on each deployment), so raw advisory water and treated water can't mix through a single leaking valve.\r\n   * Raw water from the tee runs through the trailer's **1.5\" Pressure-Sustaining Valve (`PSV-100`, set at 20 PSI)** into the **550-gal Air-Gap Break Tank (`TK-100`)**.\r\n2. **Option 1B — Fire Hydrant Supply (≥ 20 PSI Residual):**\r\n   * Uses a 2.5\" hydrant adapter, utility meter/backflow assembly, and `PSV-100` (set at 20 PSI) feeding `TK-100` through an air gap.\r\n3. **Option 1C — Potable Tanker Delivery (When Main Has Zero Pressure):**\r\n   * Because a typical restaurant only consumes **4,000 to 8,000 gallons per day**, a **single 4,000-gallon potable water tanker drop** (or a 2,000-gal nursery tank paired with `TK-100`) can keep a restaurant kitchen running for an entire shift during a complete water main break!\r\n\r\n### Stage 2: Raw Intake & Pre-Filtration (35–40 GPM)\r\n* **Raw Intake Pump (`P-101`):** Single 3 HP 316SS end-suction centrifugal pump on a VFD (40 GPM @ 55 PSI), drawing from `TK-100` via 2\" camlock suction hose (`LIT-100` provides low-level pump protection).\r\n* **Automatic Self-Cleaning Strainer (`STR-101`):** 1.5\" motorized brush strainer (`200 μm` 316SS screen) to catch rust scale and pipe debris stirred up by main breaks.\r\n* **Duplex #2 Bag / Cartridge Housings (`BF-101A/B`):** Two 316L SS single-bag #2 housings in parallel (1 Duty + 1 Standby, `25 μm` nominal).\r\n  * *Restaurant Taste & Odor Bonus:* Either housing can accept a **high-flow radial-flow activated carbon block cartridge** (`#2` size, NSF-42/61) when operating on chlorinated city water during a Boil Water Advisory, polishing off musty pipe odors before coffee/ice/beverage use without the weight of heavy GAC pressure vessels.\r\n* **Coagulant Micro-Flocculation (`P-501` + `SM-101`):** Peristaltic dosing pump injects 0.5–5 mg/L NSF-60 polyaluminum chloride (`PACl`) **downstream** of the bag filters into a 1.5\" 316L SS static mixer (`SM-101`) when raw turbidity exceeds 5 NTU.\r\n\r\n### Stage 3: Compact Hollow-Fiber Ultrafiltration (`UF-201A/B`)\r\n* **Module Count & Vertical Clearance:** **4 × 60-inch (1.5 m) industrial PVDF hollow-fiber UF modules** (`0.02 μm` nominal pore size, outside-in flow, ~550 ft² active area per module = **2,200 ft² total membrane area**), arranged in **2 independent sub-trains of 2 modules each (`Train A` & `Train B`)**.\r\n  * Using 60-inch modules instead of 79-inch hospital modules allows the entire UF skid to stand vertically with a **6.5-ft overall frame height**, fitting comfortably inside a standard **7.5-ft interior height trailer**!\r\n* **Conservative Flux:** At **35 GPM net (37 GPM gross)** across 2,200 ft², the operating flux is **24.2 GFD** — keeping transmembrane pressure (`TMP`) at `8–22 PSI` and allowing one sub-train (2 modules) to sustain 25 GPM while the other sub-train backwashes for 40 seconds.\r\n* **Automated Direct Integrity Testing (`DIT-201`):** Built-in oil-less air manifold runs an EPA Membrane Filtration Guidance Manual pressure-decay test at startup and every 24 hours (`≥ 4.0-log` Giardia and Cryptosporidium removal credit, NSF/ANSI 419 challenge-tested).\r\n* **Continuous Laser Nephelometer (`AIT-201`):** Hach TU5300sc online laser turbidimeter monitors UF filtrate (`< 0.05 NTU` normal; automatic divert to waste at `> 0.15 NTU` for 5 seconds).\r\n\r\n### Stage 4: 100% Redundant UV Disinfection (`UV-401A/B`)\r\n* **Configuration:** **2 × 40 GPM 316L SS low-pressure high-output (LPHO) amalgam UV reactors (`UV-401A` Duty + `UV-401B` 100% Standby)**, third-party validated under the **EPA UV Disinfection Guidance Manual (UVDGM)** for **≥ 40 mJ/cm² RED** at 40 GPM and 85% UVT.\r\n* **Online UVT Monitor (`AIT-401`) & Duty-Swap:** If `UV-401A` UV intensity drops or a lamp faults, `UV-401B` opens and takes over automatically without interrupting kitchen water pressure.\r\n\r\n### Stage 5: Chlorination, Segregated Chemical Cabinets & 600-Gal Contact Tank (`TK-401`)\r\n* **Segregated Chemical Storage & Ventilation:**\r\n  * Two separate, vented, bunded polyethylene safety cabinets at the rear curbside wall (`17–21 ft`):\r\n    * **Cabinet A (Oxidizer):** 15-gal day tank of 12.5% sodium hypochlorite (`NaOCl`) + dual redundant peristaltic dosing pumps (`P-502A/B`).\r\n    * **Cabinet B (Acid / Reductant):** 5-gal citric acid tote (`P-504` for UF CIP) and 5-gal sodium bisulfite tote (`P-503` for waste dechlorination), physically separated so an oxidizer and acid leak can never mix to generate chlorine gas.\r\n  * Equipped with a **wall-mounted `Cl₂` gas sensor (`AIT-901`)**, continuous 150 CFM exterior exhaust fan, and an **ANSI Z358.1 plumbed eyewash/drench hose** supplied with tempered clearwell water.\r\n* **Measured 4-Log Virus `CT` Verification (`TK-401`):**\r\n  * Chlorinated permeate flows through a 2\" white NSF-61 hose into **`TK-401`**, a **600-gallon collapsible baffled NSF-61 potable contact tank** deployed on the ground beside the trailer (footprint: `5.5 ft × 5.5 ft`).\r\n  * **CT Math at 35 GPM Rated Flow:**\r\n    * **Hydraulic Detention Time (`HDT`):** `600 gal ÷ 35 GPM = 17.1 min`\r\n    * **Effective Contact Time (`T10` with `0.50` serpentine baffling factor):** `17.1 min × 0.50 = 8.57 min`\r\n    * **Achieved `CT` at `1.5 mg/L` Free Chlorine:** `1.5 mg/L × 8.57 min = 12.85 mg·min/L`\r\n    * Even in near-freezing water (**0.5 °C, pH 6–9**), the EPA SWTR 4-log virus requirement is **12.0 mg·min/L** (and at typical 15 °C restaurant tap water, required CT is only **4.0 mg·min/L**, giving a **3.2× safety margin**).\r\n  * **Contact Tank Transfer Pump (`P-401`):** A dedicated 1.5 HP 316SS VFD pump draws CT-verified water from `TK-401` past the **`AIT-601` Free Chlorine & pH Analyzer** into the onboard clearwell `TK-601`.\r\n\r\n### Stage 6: Onboard 150-Gal Surge Clearwell & 50 GPM Duplex Kitchen Booster\r\n* **Onboard Clearwell (`TK-601`):** **150-gallon heavy-wall NSF-61 HDPE tank** with internal anti-slosh baffles, mounted directly over the tandem axles.\r\n  * *Why 150 gallons on board?* While the treatment plant runs steadily at 35 GPM through the contact tank, a restaurant kitchen experiences short 1–3 minute bursts of **45–50 GPM** when the dish machine fills while toilets flush and prep sinks run. The 150-gal clearwell provides a 10-minute surge cushion for a 15 GPM deficit so the contact tank (`TK-401`) never gets pulled faster than its rated `T10` flow!\r\n* **Duplex VFD Booster Skid (`P-601A/B`):** **2 × 3 HP vertical multi-stage 316SS centrifugal pumps** (`2 × 25 GPM = 50 GPM` combined capacity @ `60–75 PSI`, or `30 GPM` single-pump redundancy), modulated by remote building pressure transducer **`PT-603`**.\r\n* **Potable Cross-Connection Protection (`RPZ-602`):** **2\" ASSE 1013 Lead-Free Bronze/316SS Reduced Pressure Zone (RPZ) backflow preventer** inside the heated trailer prior to the 2\" and 1.5\" rear potable discharge bulkheads.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Trailer Layout (`MWTP-50-GN22`: 16-ft Main Deck + 6-ft Gooseneck Deck)\r\n\r\n* **Main Box Dimensions:** `16 ft` main deck length × `8.0 ft` exterior width (`7.5 ft` interior width) × `7.5 ft` interior ceiling height (about `10 ft 3 in` overall road height). Many drive-thru clearance bars are 9'0\"–9'6\", so the trailer stays out of drive-thru lanes and covered canopies; overhead clearance is checked on every site survey.\r\n* **Gooseneck Nose Deck (`0–6 ft`):** `6 ft` elevated deck over the pickup bed (`22 ft` total trailer length), separated from the main treatment room by a **1-hour fire-rated & acoustic bulkhead wall** (`< 65 dBA` inside the treatment bay).\r\n* **Stabilization:** Four heavy-duty 5,000-lb electric/hydraulic corner stabilizer jacks + dual 12,000-lb gooseneck landing gear.\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TB\r\n    subgraph GN[\"Gooseneck Deck (0 to 6 ft — Isolated Behind 1-Hr Fire/Acoustic Wall)\"]\r\n        direction LR\r\n        GEN[\"20 kW Tier-4 Diesel Gen + 40-Gal Tank + 240V 50A Shore Inlet + ATS\"]\r\n        AIR[\"Duplex Oil-Less Air Compressors (CMP-801A/B) + 20-Gal Receiver\"]\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    subgraph MAIN[\"16-ft Main Treatment Deck (6 to 22 ft — 30-inch Center Walkway)\"]\r\n        direction TB\r\n        subgraph FRONT[\"Front Zone (6 to 11 ft)\"]\r\n            direction LR\r\n            S12[\"Curbside: P-101 Intake (3 HP), STR-101 Strainer & Duplex #2 Bags (BF-101A/B)\"]\r\n            LAB[\"Roadside: CP-701 PLC/HMI, Fold-Down Wet Test Bench, AIT Analyzers & Eyewash\"]\r\n        end\r\n        subgraph MID[\"Mid Zone Over Tandem Axles (11 to 17 ft — Heavy Wet Load)\"]\r\n            direction LR\r\n            UF_SKID[\"Curbside: UF-201A/B (4 × 60'' Modules) + DIT-201 + P-401 CT Return Pump\"]\r\n            CW_SKID[\"Center/Roadside Over Axles: TK-601 (150-Gal Clearwell) + UV-401A/B + P-601A/B Booster\"]\r\n        end\r\n        subgraph REAR[\"Rear Zone & Bulkhead (17 to 22 ft)\"]\r\n            direction LR\r\n            CHEM[\"Curbside: Segregated Vented Chem Cabinets (NaOCl vs. Citric/Bisulfite) + AIT-901\"]\r\n            BULK[\"Roadside & Rear Doors: RPZ-602 (2''), 5 Automated Bulkhead Ports & Stowed 550/600-Gal Tanks\"]\r\n        end\r\n        FRONT --> MID --> REAR\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    GN ===|\"1-Hr Fire & Acoustic Bulkhead\"| MAIN\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Rear Camlock Bulkhead Panel (5 Color-Coded Ports)\r\n1. **`RAW INLET A` (2\" Male Camlock — Blue):** Suction from `TK-100` (550-gal ground break tank).\r\n2. **`HYDRANT / TAP INLET` (1.5\" Male Camlock — Blue):** Raw city water inlet through onboard `PT-100` + `PSV-100` (20 PSI sustaining valve) + `XV-100` out to `TK-100` air-gap fill nozzle.\r\n3. **`CT OUT` / `CT RETURN` (2\" Camlock Pair — White):** Loop to and from the 600-gal baffled ground contact tank `TK-401`.\r\n4. **`POTABLE OUTLET A` (2\" & 1.5\" Male Camlock — White, NSF-61):** Pressurized kitchen supply (60–75 PSI) downstream of `RPZ-602` and fail-safe block valve `XV-602`.\r\n5. **`WASTE / BACKWASH OUTLET` (2\" Male Camlock — Gray):** Dechlorinated UF backwash and flush discharge to the restaurant's sanitary sewer cleanout (never to the grease trap, so high-flow flush water doesn't emulsify or wash out the grease interceptor!).\r\n\r\n> [!CAUTION]\r\n> **F.O.G. / Grease Interceptor Protection Rule:** Never discharge UF backwash or startup flush water (`35–60 GPM`) into the kitchen's upstream grease interceptor inlet! High-flow clear water will wash trapped fats, oils, and grease through the interceptor into the city sewer. Always connect the 2\" gray waste hose to the **downstream sanitary sewer cleanout** (on the street side of the grease trap) identified during the AmeriPro site survey.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Dual-Mode Electrical Power Budget (240V Single-Phase)\r\n\r\nUnlike the MWTP-250 (which requires 480V 3-phase for 15 HP motors), every motor on the MWTP-50 is **≤ 3 HP** and driven by a **VFD that accepts 240V single-phase input and outputs 230V 3-phase to the motor**. This allows the entire trailer to run on **standard 240V single-phase power**!\r\n\r\n| Electrical Load | Motor / Lamp Rating | Normal Running kW (35 GPM) | Peak kW (50 GPM Burst / Backwash) |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **Raw Intake Pump (`P-101`, VFD)** | 3.0 HP | 1.60 kW | 2.20 kW |\r\n| **CT Transfer Pump (`P-401`, VFD)** | 1.5 HP | 0.85 kW | 1.10 kW |\r\n| **Duplex Kitchen Booster (`P-601A/B`, VFD)** | 2 × 3.0 HP | 2.10 kW (1 pump @ 35 GPM) | 4.40 kW (2 pumps @ 50 GPM) |\r\n| **UV Reactor (`UV-401A` Duty)** | 1 × 450 W | 0.45 kW | 0.45 kW |\r\n| **Instrument Air Compressor (`CMP-801A`, Intermittent)** | 1.5 HP | 0.35 kW (avg) | 1.20 kW |\r\n| **PLC, Analyzers, Dosing Pumps & LED Lighting** | 120V Branch | 0.50 kW | 0.65 kW |\r\n| **Roof Heat Pump HVAC / Electric Freeze Heater** | 13,500 BTU | 2.40 kW | 3.10 kW |\r\n| **TOTAL ELECTRICAL DEMAND** | — | **8.25 kW (~34.4 A @ 240V)** | **13.10 kW (~54.6 A @ 240V)** |\r\n\r\n### Two Power Operating Modes\r\n1. **Mode A — 240V 50A Shore Power Plug (Boil Water Advisory with Grid Power On):**\r\n   * Plugs into a pre-installed exterior **240V 50A twist-lock receptacle** at the restaurant.\r\n   * When switched to `50A SHORE MODE` on the HMI, the PLC caps simultaneous peak draw at **40 Amps (9.6 kW)** by pausing HVAC compressor cycling during brief 45-second backwashes — running **100% silently with $0 fuel burn**.\r\n2. **Mode B — Onboard 20 kW Tier-4 Diesel Generator (Power Outage or No Shore Plug):**\r\n   * Ultra-quiet liquid-cooled **20 kW (1,800 RPM) Tier-4 Final diesel generator** (`62 dBA @ 23 ft`) mounted on the 6-ft elevated gooseneck deck.\r\n   * **Fuel Consumption:** At 8.25 kW average load (~41% load), burns **0.85 gal/hr**. The **40-gallon belly tank** provides **47 hours of continuous runtime** between refuelings.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Itemized Bill of Materials (BOM) & Turnkey Cost Breakdown (`MWTP-50-GN22`)\r\n\r\n*Note: Budget estimates for one **MWTP-50-GN22** unit. Obtain formal vendor quotes prior to final loan underwriting.*\r\n\r\n| Category | Subsystem Description | Equipment Cost (USD) | % of Hardware |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **1.0** | **22-ft Tandem-Axle Compact Gooseneck Trailer (`16-ft` Main + `6-ft` Neck, 9,990 lb GVWR), FRP Walls, 1-Hr Firewall & 4 Corner Jacks** | $31,000 | 18.1% |\r\n| **2.0** | **Stage 1 & 2: Hydrant/Tap Kit (`PSV-100`), 550-Gal Break Tank (`TK-100`), 3 HP Intake (`P-101`), 1.5\" Auto-Strainer & Duplex #2 Bag/Carbon Housings** | $15,200 | 8.9% |\r\n| **3.0** | **Stage 3: Compact Hollow-Fiber UF Skid (4 × 60\" PVDF Modules, 2,200 ft²) + Automated `DIT-201` Skid** | $26,500 | 15.5% |\r\n| **4.0** | **Stage 4 & 5: 2 × 40 GPM UV Reactors (`UV-401A/B`), Dosing Pumps, Segregated Chem Cabinets, 600-Gal Contact Tank (`TK-401`) & `P-401`** | $23,400 | 13.7% |\r\n| **5.0** | **Stage 6: 150-Gal Onboard Clearwell (`TK-601`), Duplex 3 HP VFD Booster (`P-601A/B`) & 2\" ASSE 1013 RPZ (`RPZ-602`)** | $14,800 | 8.6% |\r\n| **6.0** | **316L SS Process Piping (1.5\"–2\"), 12 Actuated Ball Valves (`XV`), Camlock Bulkhead & Sanitized Hose Kit** | $16,500 | 9.6% |\r\n| **7.0** | **UL-508A PLC/HMI (`CP-701`), Online Laser Turbidimeter (`AIT-201`), Free Cl₂/pH (`AIT-402/601`), Cl₂ Gas Sensor & Eyewash** | $22,400 | 13.1% |\r\n| **8.0** | **20 kW Tier-4 Diesel Generator (1,800 RPM), 40-Gal Tank, 50A Shore Inlet/ATS & Duplex Oil-Less Air (`CMP-801A/B`)** | $21,700 | 12.7% |\r\n| — | **SUBTOTAL — MAJOR EQUIPMENT & MATERIALS** | **$171,500** | **100.0%** |\r\n| **9.0** | **System Integration, 316L SS Sanitary/Socket TIG Welding, Panel Wiring & Factory Acceptance Test (FAT)** | $24,000 | — |\r\n| **10.0** | **Engineering: PE Basis-of-Design Report, Stamped P&IDs & State Emergency-Use Plan Submittal** | $12,000 | — |\r\n| **11.0** | **Contingency (15% of items 1.0–10.0)** | $31,125 | — |\r\n| — | **TOTAL TURNKEY BUILD COST (Single `MWTP-50-GN22` Unit)** | **$238,625** | — |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 8. Commercial Model & Unit Economics for AmeriPro Restaurant Accounts\r\n\r\n### 8.1 Total Capital Requirement & SBA Financing\r\nBecause AmeriPro may already have 3/4-ton or 1-ton pickups in its fleet, we show both **with a dedicated new 1-ton SRW tow truck** (with factory gooseneck prep) and **using an existing fleet truck**:\r\n\r\n| Financing Scenario | Turnkey Trailer | Tow Vehicle (F-350 SRW Gooseneck) | Working Capital & Spares | Total Project Cost | 15% Equity | 85% SBA Loan (8.75%, 10 Yr) | Annual Debt Service |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **Option A: With Dedicated New 1-Ton Truck** | $238,625 | $62,000 | $24,375 | **$325,000** | $48,750 | $276,250 | **$41,547 / yr** ($3,462/mo) |\r\n| **Option B: Using Existing AmeriPro Pickup** | $238,625 | $0 (Fleet) | $21,375 | **$260,000** | $39,000 | $221,000 | **$33,238 / yr** ($2,770/mo) |\r\n\r\n### 8.2 Who Buys This, and Restaurant-Friendly Pricing\r\n**Most restaurants don't have to close during a Boil Water Advisory.** Health departments usually let them stay open on workarounds — bottled water, bagged ice, disposable service ware, and a limited menu — with ice machines, fountain drinks and some prep shut down. The `MWTP-50` is worth the most where those workarounds break down or cost more than the deployment:\r\n* **High-volume full-service restaurants and hotel kitchens** (warewashing, ice and beverage volume that bottled water can't cover).\r\n* **Multi-unit chains and franchise groups** with brand standards that don't allow a degraded menu.\r\n* **Institutional kitchens** — nursing homes, assisted living, schools, and correctional facilities — that can't send residents elsewhere.\r\n* **Food processors and commissaries** whose production lines stop without potable water.\r\n\r\n1. **Annual \"KitchenShield\" Standby Retainer (Bundled with AmeriPro F.O.G. / Hood Accounts):**\r\n   * **$1,800 / year** per single location ($150/month added to the monthly hood/grease invoice).\r\n   * **$1,500 / year per location** for multi-unit groups of 2 or more stores ($125/month per store).\r\n   * **Capacity cap:** each `MWTP-50` unit carries **no more than 10 covered locations per region**, ranked in call order that is written into every agreement. One unit serves one kitchen at a time, so a regional advisory reaches retainer holders in that order; more covered locations means another unit (§8.4).\r\n   * *Includes:* Initial plumbing & backflow site survey, pre-installed raw-supply tee and camlock adapter kit, pre-written Health Department Alternate Water Operations Plan, and annual check.\r\n2. **Emergency Mobilization & Hose Sanitization Fee:**\r\n   * **$2,500 flat fee** (covers towing, ground tank setup, 50 mg/L hose disinfection, DIT test, and initial Bac-T lab sample).\r\n3. **Active Emergency Treatment Rate:**\r\n   * **$3,200 per 24-hour day.** Non-retainer customers can also call for service at the same rates when a unit is free.\r\n   * **Staffing model:** a state-certified operator is on site through the restaurant's service hours (about 12 hours). Overnight, when the kitchen is closed, the unit runs **unattended on cellular telemetry** with automatic outlet lockout (`XV-602`) on any alarm and an on-call operator who responds within one hour. The state's emergency-use approval sets the final attendance requirement.\r\n   * **Direct Daily Operating Cost to AmeriPro:** **$749 / day** on generator ($480 attended operator shift + $100 overnight on-call/telemetry = $580/day labor; 20 gal diesel = $80/day; chemicals & Bac-T lab = $89/day), or **$669 / day** on restaurant shore power → **76.6%–79.1% direct margin ($2,451–$2,531/day net contribution)**.\r\n\r\n### 8.3 Standalone 3-Year Pro-Forma & DSCR (Single `MWTP-50-GN22` Unit)\r\n\r\n| Financial Metric | Year 1 (8 Covered Locations, 25 Active Days) | Year 2 (10 Locations — Cap, 45 Active Days) | Year 3 (10 Locations — Cap, 60 Active Days) |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **Annual Standby Retainers ($1,800 single / $1,500 per group store)** | $12,900 *(3 single + 5 group)* | $16,200 *(4 single + 6 group)* | $16,200 *(4 single + 6 group)* |\r\n| **Mobilization Fees ($2,500 × deployments)** | $15,000 (6 events) | $25,000 (10 events) | $35,000 (14 events) |\r\n| **Active Treatment Days ($3,200 / day)** | $80,000 (25 days) | $144,000 (45 days) | $192,000 (60 days) |\r\n| **GROSS REVENUE** | **$107,900** | **$185,200** | **$243,200** |\r\n| **Direct Deployment COGS ($749/day + $450/mob)** | ($21,425) | ($38,205) | ($51,240) |\r\n| **Fixed Overhead (Insurance, Storage, Annual Membrane/UV Service)** | ($28,000) | ($29,500) | ($31,000) |\r\n| **NET OPERATING INCOME (EBITDA)** | **$58,475** | **$117,495** | **$160,960** |\r\n| **Annual Debt Service (Option A w/ New Truck: $276.25k Loan)** | $41,547 | $41,547 | $41,547 |\r\n| **DEBT SERVICE COVERAGE RATIO (DSCR — Option A)** | **1.41×** | **2.83×** | **3.87×** |\r\n| **DEBT SERVICE COVERAGE RATIO (DSCR — Option B Existing Truck)** | **1.76×** | **3.53×** | **4.84×** |\r\n\r\n> [!TIP]\r\n> **What carries the loan:** Retainers are steady, but with the 10-location cap they cover about **20% of fixed costs plus debt service** — deployments carry the rest. About **seven 3-day restaurant deployments a year** (Option B, existing truck) cover all fixed costs and the loan payment with no retainers at all. Every deployment also builds AmeriPro's state regulatory track record toward the hospital-grade `MWTP-250`.\r\n\r\n### 8.4 Growing Into a Fleet\r\nBecause one unit serves one kitchen at a time, coverage grows by adding units, not by selling more retainers on the same trailer. Each additional `MWTP-50` ($238,625 turnkey, towed by an existing fleet pickup) adds **10 more covered locations** and its own deployment capacity, and can be financed on its own track record. A fleet of two or three units also lets AmeriPro keep one unit available for walk-in emergency calls during a regional advisory.\r\n","MWTP_250_Engineering_Design_Package.md":"# MWTP-250 Engineering Design Package: 36-ft Gooseneck Emergency Mobile Water Treatment Facility\r\n\r\n**Revision B — November 2026.** Changes from Rev A: raw-water break tank (no pumping from a depressurized main), measured chlorine contact time (CT), daily UF integrity testing, onboard GAC removed, pumps and generator resized, membranes kept wet in storage, segregated chemical room, Class A CDL tow configuration, corrected weights and loads.\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> **Approvals:** Fabrication follows PE-stamped drawings, plan review by the state drinking water program (Georgia EPD Drinking Water Program in our home state), and manufacturer dimension and weight sheets for every skid.\r\n\r\n## 1. Design Basis & System Overview\r\n\r\n### 1.1 Mission Profile\r\nThis system is built for two emergency conditions:\r\n\r\n1. **Boil Water Advisory (primary market):** the municipal main still has water and pressure (≥ 20 PSI residual), but the water can't be trusted after a main break, pressure loss, or storm damage. The trailer draws from a hydrant through a break tank, treats the water, and pressurizes the customer's building.\r\n2. **Water outage (secondary market):** the main has no usable pressure. A depressurized main has no water to give, so the trailer is fed by a **tanker shuttle** or an approved **surface/well source** into the same break tank. At 250 GPM the plant needs about **15,000 gallons per hour** of raw water, so the tanker plan must be arranged in advance.\r\n\r\n- **Primary Targets:** Hospitals, acute-care clinics, commercial kitchens/restaurants, and emergency community distribution points.\r\n- **Mobility Platform:** **36-ft Enclosed Triple-Axle Gooseneck Trailer** (28 ft main deck + 8 ft gooseneck deck), towed by a **Class 5 chassis-cab truck** (F-550 / Ram 5500 class with gooseneck flatbed). No semi-tractor is needed, but the combination is over 26,000 lb GCWR with a trailer over 10,000 lb GVWR, so **the driver must hold a Class A CDL** and the company needs a USDOT number.\r\n- **Feed Water Source:** Municipal hydrant at ≥ 20 PSI residual (with utility hydrant permit, hydrant meter, and backflow device), tanker shuttle, or an approved surface/well source — always into the raw-water break tank, never pumped directly from the main.\r\n\r\n### 1.2 Why Ultrafiltration Instead of Reverse Osmosis\r\nBecause the feed water is fresh municipal or surface water (TDS < 500 mg/L), **reverse osmosis is not required**. Ultrafiltration (UF) + UV + chlorine with measured contact time gives:\r\n1. **Higher flow in the same footprint:** 250 GPM rated, versus roughly 50–75 GPM for RO in a trailer this size.\r\n2. **~96% water recovery:** about 4% of the feed is used for UF backwashes and strainer flushes, versus 25%–40% RO reject.\r\n3. **Much lower pressure and power:** UF runs at 30–55 PSI instead of 200–400 PSI. The whole plant, including building booster pumps, runs on an **80 kW** generator.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 1.3 Capacity at 250 GPM\r\n\r\n**Rated output:** 250 GPM = 360,000 gallons per day. **Net output** after backwashes, strainer flushes, and daily integrity tests is about **245 GPM average (~350,000 GPD)**. During each 45-second backwash one train is offline and output briefly drops to ~125 GPM; the contact tank and clearwell cover the dip.\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> The figures below are **daily-volume equivalents** to show scale. One trailer serves **one building connection at a time**, and peak instantaneous demand (not daily volume) decides whether a facility can be carried. Hospital cooling-tower makeup is excluded — towers can use several hundred GPM on their own and should be planned separately.\r\n\r\n| Emergency Facility / Scenario | Standard Demand Benchmark | Daily-Volume Equivalent |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Acute-Care Hospital** (domestic potable, sanitation, sterilization — cooling towers excluded) | 150–250 gal / bed / day | One hospital of up to several hundred beds, if its **peak** domestic demand is ≤ 250 GPM |\r\n| **Full-Service Restaurant / Commercial Kitchen** | 2,500–4,000 gal / day (peak 15–60 GPM) | One restaurant per connection; the plant is far larger than one restaurant needs (see the MWTP-50 compact restaurant unit) |\r\n| **Emergency Drinking & Sanitation (FEMA planning figure)** | 15 gal / person / day | ~23,000 people / day via the community fill manifold |\r\n| **Survival Drinking Water Only** | 1 gal / person / day | ~350,000 people / day (distribution logistics, not the plant, is the limit) |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Process Flow Diagram (PFD) & Mass Balance\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Process Architecture\r\nThe treatment train is a **6-stage multi-barrier process** with automated fail-safe monitoring:\r\n\r\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph Stage1[\"Stage 1: Supply, Break Tank & Intake (260 GPM)\"]\n        direction LR\n        HYD[\"Hydrant ≥ 20 PSI (meter + backflow)<br/>OR Tanker / Surface Source\"] --> PSV[\"PSV-100 Pressure-<br/>Sustaining Valve (20 PSI)\"]\n        PSV --> TK100[\"TK-100 3,000-Gal Raw<br/>Break Tank (air-gap fill)\"]\n        TK100 --> P101[\"P-101 Raw Intake Pump<br/>(15 HP VFD, 260 GPM)\"]\n        P101 --> AS[\"STR-101 Auto-Backwashing<br/>Strainer (200 μm)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Stage2[\"Stage 2: Pre-Filtration & Inline Coagulation\"]\n        direction LR\n        BF[\"BF-101A/B Duplex<br/>Bag Filters (25 μm)\"] --> INJ1[\"P-501 PACl Coagulant<br/>(only if turbidity > 5 NTU)\"]\n        INJ1 --> SM[\"SM-101 316SS<br/>Static Mixer\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Stage34[\"Stage 3: Parallel Hollow-Fiber UF & Stage 4: Validated UV\"]\n        direction LR\n        UF1[\"UF Train A & Train B<br/>(2 × 125 GPM, 14 PVDF Modules)\"] --> BW[\"Air Scour + Backwash +<br/>Daily Pressure-Decay DIT\"]\n        BW --> UV[\"UV-401A/B/C Validated Reactors<br/>(2 Duty + 1 Standby, ≥ 40 mJ/cm²)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Stage5[\"Stage 5: Chlorination & Measured Contact Time (CT)\"]\n        direction LR\n        INJ2[\"P-502A/B 12.5% NaOCl<br/>(flow-paced, residual-trimmed)\"] --> CT[\"TK-401 3,000-Gal Baffled<br/>Contact Tank (T10 ≥ 6 min)\"]\n        CT --> P401[\"P-401 Transfer<br/>Pump (5 HP)\"]\n        P401 --> CW[\"TK-601 500-Gal<br/>Baffled Clearwell\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Stage6[\"Stage 6: Facility Pressurization & Distribution (250 GPM)\"]\n        direction LR\n        DUP[\"P-601A/B/C Triplex VFD Booster<br/>(2 Duty + 1 Standby, 15 HP ea)\"] --> RPZ[\"RPZ-602 ASSE 1013<br/>Backflow Preventer\"]\n        RPZ --> BLDG[\"Hospital / Restaurant Domestic<br/>Tie-In (never the fire FDC)\"]\n        RPZ --> TAP[\"12-Tap Community<br/>Filling Manifold\"]\n    end\n\n    AS --> BF\n    SM --> UF1\n    UV --> INJ2\n    CW --> DUP\n```\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Disinfection Credit Basis\r\n\r\nRegulators grant log credit to specific, monitored steps — not to the number of stages. The design relies on the following, all subject to state approval:\r\n\r\n| Target | Primary Barrier | How Credit Is Earned & Proven |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Cryptosporidium / Giardia** | UF membranes + UV | UF credit is set by the module's **NSF/ANSI 419** challenge-test result and proven daily by a **pressure-decay direct integrity test (DIT)**; turbidity is continuous indirect monitoring. UV at ≥ 40 mJ/cm² in **UVDGM-validated** reactors with a UVT analyzer adds Crypto/Giardia credit. |\r\n| **Bacteria** | UF + chlorine | UF physical removal plus free chlorine. |\r\n| **Viruses** | **Chlorine contact time (CT)** | UF and UV at 40 mJ/cm² are **not** counted for virus (EPA's 4-log virus UV dose is 186 mJ/cm² based on adenovirus, and most states give UF little or no virus credit). The PLC computes CT live from flow, temperature, pH, and outlet residual and blocks the building outlet if CT falls short. |\r\n\r\n**CT check (4-log virus, free chlorine, pH 6–9):**\r\n- Required CT: about **12 mg·min/L at 0.5 °C**, 8 at 5 °C, 6 at 10 °C, 3 at 20 °C.\r\n- The baffled 3,000-gal contact tank at 250 GPM gives ~12 min hydraulic residence; at a baffle factor of 0.5, **T10 ≈ 6 min**.\r\n- At **2.0 mg/L** outlet residual: CT ≈ 12 mg·min/L → meets 4-log virus down to near freezing. At 1.5 mg/L: CT ≈ 9 → meets at 5 °C and above.\r\n- The onboard 500-gal clearwell alone (Rev A) gave only ~1–1.4 min T10, which is not enough. That is why the contact tank is now deployed on **every** job.\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Mass Balance & Hydraulic Profile (100% Load)\r\n\r\n| Stream ID | Process Node | Flow Rate (GPM) | Daily Volume (GPD) | Pressure (PSI) | Key Water Quality Target |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **S-100** | Hydrant / Tanker Fill to Break Tank `TK-100` | 260 GPM | 374,400 GPD | Hydrant ≥ 20 PSI residual (held by `PSV-100`) → atmospheric | Turbidity ≤ 50 NTU, pH 6.5–8.5 |\r\n| **S-101** | `P-101` Discharge | 260 GPM | 374,400 GPD | 55 PSI | — |\r\n| **S-102** | Post 200 μm Strainer | 258 GPM | 371,520 GPD | 53 PSI | Solids > 200 μm removed |\r\n| **S-103** | Post 25 μm Bags + PACl (UF Feed) | 258 GPM | 371,520 GPD | 48 PSI | Pipe scale/rust removed ahead of UF |\r\n| **S-104** | UF Permeate | 250 GPM rated (245 avg net) | 360,000 rated / ~350,000 net | 25 PSI | **Turbidity < 0.05 NTU**; credit per NSF 419 + daily DIT |\r\n| **S-105** | Post UV + NaOCl Injection | 250 GPM | 360,000 GPD | 18 PSI | UV dose ≥ 40 mJ/cm² |\r\n| **S-106** | Contact Tank `TK-401` Outlet | 250 GPM | 360,000 GPD | Atmospheric | Free Cl₂ 1.5–2.0 mg/L, CT ≥ required for 4-log virus |\r\n| **S-107** | **Finished Potable Discharge** (post `RPZ-602`) | **250 GPM** | **360,000 GPD** | Pump ~95–100 PSI → **65–85 PSI at building** | Free Cl₂ ≥ 1.0 mg/L at farthest tap; Bac-T samples per state |\r\n| **W-201** | All Waste (strainer, UF backwash, divert, drains) | 10 GPM (avg) | 14,400 GPD | 15 PSI | **Dechlorinated**; to sanitary sewer with utility permission |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Major Equipment Schedule & Specifications\r\n\r\n### 3.1 Supply, Break Tank & Pre-Treatment\r\n* **Hydrant Pressure-Sustaining Valve (`PSV-100`) & Pressure Transmitter (`PT-100`):**\r\n  * Installed downstream of the utility-supplied hydrant meter and backflow device. Holds the hydrant at **≥ 20 PSI residual** so the trailer can never pull the main into low or negative pressure. If residual drops below 20 PSI, `XV-100` closes and the operator switches to tanker supply.\r\n* **Raw-Water Break Tank (`TK-100`):**\r\n  * 3,000-gal open-top frame tank (NSF-61 liner, cover), ground-deployed, **filled through an air gap** with float/level control (`LIT-100`). The air gap is the backflow protection between the city system and the trailer. Tankers fill the same tank.\r\n* **Raw Water Intake Pump (`P-101`):**\r\n  * **Type:** Self-priming end-suction centrifugal, drafting from `TK-100`.\r\n  * **Capacity:** 260 GPM @ 130 ft TDH, **15 HP** VFD (≈ 8.5 water HP / ~12 BHP at 70% efficiency — Rev A's 7.5 HP was undersized), 316SS wet end.\r\n* **Automatic Self-Cleaning Strainer (`STR-101`):**\r\n  * Motorized brush/suction scanner, 200 μm 316SS wedge-wire, 3\" flanged. ΔP-triggered flush without interrupting flow.\r\n* **Duplex Pre-Filter Housing (`BF-101A/B`):**\r\n  * 2 × 4-bag 304SS housings with changeover valve. 25 μm felt bags catch pipe scale. **Bags are upstream of the coagulant** so floc can't blind them.\r\n* **Inline Coagulation (`P-501`, `SM-101`):**\r\n  * PACl dosed after the bags and mixed in `SM-101` immediately ahead of the UF (inline coagulation-UF). Used only when raw turbidity > 5 NTU or when treating surface water. There is **no pre-chlorination** in this design.\r\n\r\n### 3.2 Hollow-Fiber Ultrafiltration Skid (`UF-201A/B`)\r\n* **Configuration:** 2 parallel trains × 7 modules (14 total) + backwash pump `P-202` (180 GPM @ 40 PSI, 5 HP) + oil-free air scour blower `BL-201` + 100-gal CIP tank with 6 kW heater.\r\n* **Membrane Specs:** PVDF hollow fiber, 0.02 μm nominal, 775 ft² (72 m²) per module (10,850 ft² total). Modules must carry **NSF/ANSI 61** and an **NSF/ANSI 419** challenge-test result.\r\n* **Design Flux:** 33 GFD at 250 GPM with both trains online.\r\n* **Backwash:** 45-second air scour + reverse flush per train every 45 minutes (or on TMP). The partner train stays online, so output drops to ~125 GPM for those 45 seconds.\r\n* **Direct Integrity Test (`DIT-201`):** Daily pressure-decay test per train using regulated instrument air through `XV-207A/B`. A failed test takes that train out of service until the broken fiber is pinned. The UF trains don't earn log credit without it.\r\n* **Storage & Transport:** **Membranes are never allowed to dry out** — PVDF fibers that dry lose permeability permanently. Between jobs the modules are filled with preservative (sodium bisulfite solution, or a glycol-based preservative in freezing weather) per the membrane maker's instructions.\r\n* **Fit:** 2-meter-class modules plus headers are taller than Rev A's 7.5-ft ceiling. Rev B specifies a 9.5-ft main-deck interior (§4). Confirm module length and skid height from the maker's dimension sheets, or use horizontal-mount modules.\r\n\r\n### 3.3 UV, Chlorination & Contact Time (`UV-401`, `CHEM-501`, `TK-401`)\r\n* **Onboard GAC removed (Rev A item `GAC-301A/B`):** 50 ft³ of carbon at 250 GPM gave only ~1.5 min empty-bed contact time and ~18 gpm/ft² loading. VOC/PFAS removal needs roughly 10–20 min, which can't fit on this trailer. Removing it cuts ~2,600 lb dry / ~4,900 lb wet and frees the space the larger pumps and safety equipment need. If a job needs carbon for taste/odor, it can be added as a separate ground skid. Rev B makes **no claims** for VOC, PFAS, pesticide, or petrochemical removal.\r\n* **UV Disinfection Reactors (`UV-401A/B/C`):**\r\n  * 3 × 125 GPM LPHO amalgam 316L SS reactors, **2 duty + 1 standby** (Rev A had no spare — losing one reactor meant cutting flow in half).\r\n  * ≥ 40 mJ/cm² validated under **EPA UVDGM** (NSF/ANSI 55 Class A is a residential/point-of-entry standard and is not the basis here), with UV intensity sensors and an online **UVT analyzer** (`AIT-401`).\r\n* **Chemical Dosing Skid (`CHEM-501`):**\r\n  * **Coagulant (`P-501`):** PACl, 0.5–5.0 GPH, flow-paced to raw turbidity.\r\n  * **Disinfection (`P-502A/B`):** Duty/standby digital diaphragm pumps, flow-paced and trimmed by the contact-tank outlet chlorine analyzer (`AIT-402`).\r\n  * **Waste Dechlorination (`P-503`):** Sodium bisulfite dosed into the **common waste header**, so every waste stream — strainer flush, backwash, off-spec divert, clearwell and contact-tank drains — is dechlorinated.\r\n  * **CIP (`P-504`):** Citric acid for membrane clean-in-place. NaOCl CIP is batched from the oxidizer cabinet.\r\n* **Contact Tank (`TK-401`):** 3,000-gal NSF-61 frame tank with internal baffle curtains and a cover, deployed on the ground beside the trailer on every job. Transfer pump `P-401` (5 HP self-priming, 260 GPM @ 40 ft) returns treated water to the onboard clearwell.\r\n\r\n### 3.4 Segregated Chemical Room & Safety Station\r\n* **Separate containment:** The oxidizer (12.5% NaOCl) cabinet is physically separated from the acid/reducer cabinet (citric acid, sodium bisulfite) and the PACl cabinet, each with its own spill containment. **Acid + hypochlorite releases chlorine gas; bisulfite + hypochlorite reacts violently.**\r\n* **Ventilation & detection:** Dedicated exhaust fan for the chemical room, interlocked to a **chlorine gas detector** (`AIT-901`) with local horn/strobe and HMI alarm.\r\n* **Eyewash/drench:** ANSI Z358.1 tempered eyewash/drench station within 10 seconds' travel of the chemical room (OSHA 29 CFR 1910.151(c)). Spill kits, Class ABC and Class B extinguishers, SDS binder, chemical PPE.\r\n* **Hauling chemicals:** Bleach is carried in containers of **8 gal or less** and within the DOT **materials of trade** aggregate limit (49 CFR 173.6); bulk bleach is bought fresh near the job site. 12.5% bleach loses strength quickly in a hot trailer, so fresh stock also gives a more accurate dose.\r\n\r\n### 3.5 Clearwell & Building Pressurization System (`TK-601`, `P-601A/B/C`)\r\n* **Onboard Baffled Clearwell (`TK-601`):** 500-gal rectangular XLPE tank (~32\" × 48\" × 75\", fits the side bay) in a steel transport cage. Serves as booster suction and surge tank — **it is not counted for CT**.\r\n* **Triplex VFD Booster Skid (`P-601A/B/C`):**\r\n  * 3 × 125 GPM vertical multistage 316SS pumps, **15 HP each, 2 duty + 1 standby** (Rev A's 7.5 HP pumps were undersized).\r\n  * Sizing: 80 PSI at the building + ~12 PSI loss across a 3\" RPZ at 250 GPM + ~7 PSI in 150 ft of 3\" hose ≈ **~100 PSI (231 ft) at the pump** → ~14.6 water HP / ~22 BHP for two duty pumps.\r\n  * Individual VFDs hold constant pressure **at the building** (remote pressure transmitter `PT-603` on the building side of the hose), 5–250 GPM, with an 85-gal ASME bladder tank.\r\n\r\n### 3.6 Automated Water Quality & SCADA Control Panel (`CP-701`)\r\n* **Controller:** Allen-Bradley CompactLogix PLC, 15\" NEMA 4X touchscreen HMI, UPS, cellular/Starlink telemetry.\r\n* **Continuous online monitoring:**\r\n  * Raw & UF permeate **turbidity** (Hach TU5300sc; permeate diverts to waste above 0.15 NTU).\r\n  * **UVT** and UV intensity.\r\n  * **Free chlorine, pH & temperature** at the contact-tank outlet (`AIT-402`) for live CT calculation, and at the clearwell outlet (`AIT-601`).\r\n  * Hydrant residual (`PT-100`), break tank level (`LIT-100`), TMP, DIT pressure decay, building pressure (`PT-602`/`PT-603`), instrument air pressure, chlorine gas detector.\r\n* **Sampling:** Total coliform (Bac-T) sample taps at the clearwell outlet and building end; samples go to a state-certified lab per the state's emergency-use requirements.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. 36-ft Gooseneck Trailer Layout & Weight Distribution\r\n\r\n### 4.1 Spatial Architecture (Top-Down Schematic)\r\n- **Total Length:** 36 ft (8 ft gooseneck + 28 ft main deck)\r\n- **Interior Width:** 8.0 ft (96\" inside insulated walls; 102\" legal exterior). With a 32\" center aisle each side bay is **32\" deep** — equipment must be ≤ 30\" deep, or straddle the aisle at the rear.\r\n- **Interior Height:** **9.5 ft main deck** (custom tall box within 13'-6\" legal height — Rev A's 7.5 ft won't clear UF modules or the clearwell). The gooseneck deck is an **exterior-access machinery compartment**, not a crew space.\r\n- **Axles:** Triple 10,000 lb torsion axles, electric-over-hydraulic disc brakes (**30,000 lb GVWR**) + 4-point hydraulic leveling outriggers.\r\n\r\n```\r\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\r\n| GOOSENECK MACHINERY COMPARTMENT  |                   28 FT MAIN DECK (9.5 FT CLEAR)              |\r\n| (fire-separated, exterior doors) |                                                               |\r\n| +---------------+ +------------+ | +-------------+  +----------------------+  +----------------+ |\r\n| | 80 kW DIESEL  | | DUPLEX AIR | | | PLC / HMI   |  |  UF SKID TRAINS A/B  |  | 500-GAL BAFFLED| |\r\n| |  GENERATOR    | | COMPRESSORS| | | ANALYZERS   |  |  14 PVDF MODULES     |  |  CLEARWELL     | |\r\n| | (Sound Atten, | | + RECEIVER | | | WET LAB     |  |  + DIT PANEL         |  |  (TK-601)      | |\r\n| |  radiator &   | |  + DRYER   | | | + EYEWASH   |  |  [OVER TRIPLE AXLES] |  |                | |\r\n| |  exhaust vent | +------------+ | +-------------+  +----------------------+  +----------------+ |\r\n| |  to exterior) |                |  [Side Door]                                                  |\r\n| | + 150 Gal     |   [Firewall]   |===================[32\" CENTER WALKWAY]========================|\r\n| |  Belly Tank   |                | +-------------+  +-----------+ +--------+  +----------------+ |\r\n| |               |                | | INTAKE PUMP |  | UV x3     | |CHEM RM |  |  TRIPLEX VFD   | |\r\n| | [Front Louver]|                | | AUTO STRAIN |  | BW PUMP   | |(vented,|  | BOOSTERS + RPZ | |\r\n| |               |                | | & BAG FILT  |  | CIP TANK  | | Cl2 det)|  | + P-401 XFER  | |\r\n| +---------------+                | +-------------+  +-----------+ +--------+  +----------------+ |\r\n+----------------------------------+------------------+----------------------+---------------------+\r\n   Belly boxes: TK-100 raw break tank + TK-401 contact tank (folded), hose kit, hydrant kit\r\n                                                      |<--- TRIPLE AXLES --->|  [Rear Manifold Door]\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 4.2 Road vs. Wet Operating Weight Budget\r\n\r\n> [!IMPORTANT]\r\n> Before towing, the clearwell, piping, and ground tanks are drained and the UF modules are **filled with preservative** (not blown dry). The road weight therefore includes the water held in the modules.\r\n\r\n| Subsystem / Zone | Location on Trailer | Road Weight (lbs) | Wet Operating Weight on Trailer (lbs) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **36-ft Triple-Axle Enclosed Gooseneck, 9.5-ft tall box** (R-12 insulation, firewall, HVAC, chemical-room exhaust, hydraulic jacks) | Full Chassis | 9,800 | 9,800 |\r\n| **80 kW Diesel Generator + 150-Gal Fuel + Duplex Air Compressors** | Gooseneck Compartment | 4,400 | 4,400 |\r\n| **PLC Cabinet, Analyzers, Wet Lab, Eyewash/Safety Station** | Front Main Deck | 700 | 750 |\r\n| **Intake Pump (15 HP), Auto-Strainer, Duplex Bag Filter, 316SS Piping** | Forward-Mid Deck | 1,350 | 1,750 |\r\n| **UF Skid (14 Modules preserved wet), DIT Panel, BW Pump, CIP Tank** | **Directly Over Triple Axles** | 3,300 | 3,900 |\r\n| **3 UV Reactors + Segregated Chemical Room (MOT quantities)** | Over Axles / Rear-Mid | 1,100 | 1,500 |\r\n| **500-Gal Clearwell + Triplex 15 HP Booster + P-401 + RPZ** | Rear Deck | 2,150 | 6,320 |\r\n| **Stowed TK-100 & TK-401 Frame Tanks + Hose & Hydrant Kit** | Belly Boxes | 1,300 | 0 (deployed on ground) |\r\n| **TOTAL WEIGHT** | **GVWR Limit: 30,000 lbs** | **24,100 lbs** | **28,420 lbs** |\r\n\r\n* **Pin Weight:** ~5,300 lbs (≈ 22% of road weight). That is more than the payload of most one-ton and F-450-class pickups, so the tow vehicle is a **Class 5 chassis cab with a gooseneck flatbed**.\r\n* **Licensing:** Trailer GVWR 30,000 lb + truck GVWR ≈ 19,500 lb → GCWR ≈ 49,500 lb. Over 26,000 lb GCWR with a trailer over 10,000 lb GVWR = **Class A CDL**, plus USDOT number, DOT medical cards, hours-of-service, drug & alcohol testing, and annual inspection. Disaster declarations can relax hours-of-service, but not the CDL requirement.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Electrical Load & Autonomous Power Budget\r\n\r\nThe plant runs **off-grid** on its onboard generator, or on **shore power** (480V 3-phase, 200A camlock input through the ATS) where a utility or temporary service is available. Hospital emergency generators feed life-safety and critical branches under NFPA 99/110, so don't plan on connecting to them without the hospital engineer's written approval. The ATS is open-transition: expect a brief interruption when it transfers.\r\n\r\n| Equipment Item | HP / Rating | Running Load (kW) | Duty Cycle | Continuous Avg (kW) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Raw Water Intake Pump (`P-101`, VFD) | 15 HP | 9.7 kW | 100% | 9.7 kW |\r\n| Contact Tank Transfer Pump (`P-401`, VFD) | 5 HP | 3.7 kW | 100% | 3.7 kW |\r\n| Triplex Building Booster (`P-601`, 2 duty VFD at 250 GPM) | 2 × 15 HP | 17.8 kW | 100% | 17.8 kW |\r\n| UF Backwash Pump (`P-202`) + Air Scour Blower (`BL-201`) | 5 HP + 2 HP | 5.2 kW | 5% | 0.3 kW |\r\n| UV Reactors (2 duty of 3) | 2 × 1.2 kW | 2.4 kW | 100% | 2.4 kW |\r\n| Duplex Instrument Air Compressors (lead/lag) + Dryer | 2 × 2 HP | 1.5 kW | 25% | 0.4 kW |\r\n| PLC, Analyzers, Metering Pumps, LED Lighting | 120V Subpanel | 1.5 kW | 100% | 1.5 kW |\r\n| Trailer HVAC + Chemical-Room Exhaust | 2.8 kW | 2.8 kW | 70% | 2.0 kW |\r\n| Strainer Drive Motor | 0.5 HP | 0.4 kW | 5% | 0.02 kW |\r\n| CIP Tank Heater (only during CIP, not during production) | 6 kW | 6.0 kW | 0% in production | 0 kW |\r\n| **TOTAL ELECTRICAL DEMAND** | — | **Peak in production: 45.0 kW** (51 kW with CIP heater) | — | **Avg: 37.8 kW** |\r\n\r\n* **Generator Sizing:** **80 kW prime Tier 4 Final diesel** — about 47% average and 56% peak load. Variable-frequency drives draw harmonic current, and generator makers typically recommend oversizing for VFD loads; the margin also covers motor starts and keeps the Tier 4 aftertreatment (DPF/DEF) above its light-load problems.\r\n* **Fuel Autonomy:** ~3.1 gal/hr at 37.8 kW → the 150-gal belly tank gives **about 48 hours** at 250 GPM, plus external fuel-tank quick-connects.\r\n* **Single points of failure:** One generator and one plant. For hospital service, keep shore-power capability and have a backup rental generator source on call.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Hospital & Restaurant Emergency Hookup Architecture\r\n\r\n### 6.1 Rear Exterior Connection Bulkhead\r\nAll connections are behind an insulated, heated rear compartment door, using **NSF-61 stainless camlock fittings**:\r\n1. **RAW INLET A:** 4\" camlock from the break tank `TK-100` (suction).\r\n2. **TANK FILL OUTLET:** 4\" hose from the hydrant kit (utility hydrant meter + backflow + `PSV-100` + `XV-100`) to the air-gap fill on `TK-100`. Tankers discharge into the same tank.\r\n3. **CONTACT TANK OUT/RETURN:** 4\" camlocks to and from `TK-401`.\r\n4. **OUTLET A (Hospital Tie-In):** 3\" female camlock (post-RPZ) to the hospital's **pre-installed emergency domestic water connection**, surveyed and fitted during the retainer site visit.\r\n5. **OUTLET B (Restaurant / Commercial Kitchen Tie-In):** 1.5\" & 2\" NSF-61 connections to a restaurant's domestic water connection, pre-surveyed with the owner, plumber, and utility.\r\n6. **OUTLET C (Community Distribution Manifold):** Fold-out manifold with **12 × 3/4\" hose bibs** for filling jugs.\r\n7. **WASTE OUTLET:** 3\" camlock — dechlorinated waste to a **sanitary sewer cleanout with the utility's permission**. Storm drains are used only with written MS4/state permission and never for coagulant-laden backwash.\r\n\r\n> [!CAUTION]\r\n> **Never connect to a fire department connection (FDC).** FDCs feed stagnant fire sprinkler piping, not the domestic potable system.\r\n\r\n### 6.2 Deployment Timeline (Arrival to Building Water in About 3 Hours at a Pre-Surveyed Site)\r\n1. **0:00–0:30 — Position, stabilize & power:** Set up traffic control, chock, level on the jacks, start the generator.\r\n2. **0:30–1:15 — Supply & ground tanks:** Deploy `TK-100` and `TK-401`. Install the utility hydrant meter, backflow, and `PSV-100`; confirm ≥ 20 PSI residual (or start the tanker shuttle). Connect waste to sanitary sewer.\r\n3. **1:15–2:15 — Start-up & prove:** Startup flush to waste, UF direct integrity test, fill and turn over the contact tank, confirm turbidity < 0.05 NTU, UV dose, and CT. Sanitize the delivery hose with the hose-sanitizing kit.\r\n4. **2:15–3:00 — Facility pressurization:** Utility/facility closes the building's service valve, hose connects to the pre-installed tie-in, soft-ramp fill at 30 PSI, then normal pressure. Bac-T samples are pulled and handled per the state's emergency approval.\r\n\r\nA first-time site without a pre-installed connection will take longer and needs a licensed plumber and the utility.\r\n","MWTP_250_PID_and_Valve_Matrix.md":"# MWTP-250 Detailed Piping & Instrumentation Diagram (P&ID) & Automated Valve Matrix\r\n\r\n**Revision B — November 2026.** Adds the raw break tank, contact tank, UF direct integrity test, third UV reactor, and duplex instrument air; removes the GAC vessels (`XV-301`, `XV-302`); reorders coagulant injection after the bag filters; moves dechlorination to the common waste header.\r\n\r\n## 1. Piping & Instrumentation Diagram (P&ID)\r\n\r\n### 1.1 Tagging & Line Designation Legend (ISA-5.1 Standard)\r\n* **Process Lines:**\r\n  * `RW-4\"-316SS`: 4\" Raw Water Header (break tank to intake pump; Schedule 10 316L)\r\n  * `PF-3\"-316SS`: 3\" Pre-Filtered / Coagulated UF Feed Header\r\n  * `UF-3\"-316SS`: 3\" Ultrafiltration Permeate Header\r\n  * `CT-4\"-HOSE`: 4\" NSF-61 hose to/from ground contact tank `TK-401`\r\n  * `PW-3\"-316SS`: 3\" Finished Potable Water Header (NSF-61)\r\n  * `BW-3\"-PVC80`: 3\" Common Waste Header (Schedule 80 PVC, dechlorinated)\r\n  * `IA-1\"-316SS`: 1\" Oil-Free Instrument / Scour / Integrity-Test Air Header (80 PSI)\r\n* **Valve Prefixes:**\r\n  * `XV-xxx`: Pneumatically actuated butterfly/ball valve (24VDC solenoid pilot, spring return)\r\n  * `HV-xxx`: Manual isolation / changeover valve\r\n  * `PSV-xxx`: Pressure-sustaining valve\r\n  * `RV-xxx`: Pressure relief / vacuum breaker\r\n  * `CV-xxx`: Spring-loaded silent check valve\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 1.2 Master System P&ID Schematic\n\n> **Tip:** Use the **Zoom (`+` / `−`), Fit, Drag-to-Pan, or Fullscreen (`⛶`)** controls in the top-right corner of each schematic below to inspect individual valve tags and instrument loops.\n\n#### Sheet 1.2A — Stages 1, 2 & 3: Supply, Pre-Filtration & Parallel Hollow-Fiber UF\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph S1[\"STAGE 1: RAW SUPPLY, BREAK TANK & INTAKE\"]\n        direction LR\n        HYD[\"Hydrant (meter + backflow)<br/>or Potable Tanker\"] --> PT100([\"PT-100 Residual<br/>(hold ≥ 20 PSI)\"])\n        PT100 --> PSV100[\"PSV-100 Pressure-<br/>Sustaining Valve\"]\n        PSV100 --> XV100[\"XV-100 Break Tank<br/>Fill Valve\"]\n        XV100 -- \"Air Gap\" --> TK100[\"TK-100 3,000-Gal<br/>Raw Break Tank (LIT-100)\"]\n        TK100 --> HV101[\"HV-101 Iso +<br/>PSL/PT-101 Suction\"]\n        HV101 --> P101[\"P-101 Intake Pump<br/>(15 HP VFD, 260 GPM) + CV-101\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph S2[\"STAGE 2: AUTO-STRAINER, DUPLEX BAG FILTERS & INLINE COAGULATION\"]\n        direction LR\n        AIT101([\"AIT-101 Raw<br/>Turbidity & pH\"]) --> STR101[\"STR-101 200 μm<br/>Auto Strainer\"]\n        STR101 -- \"PDI-102 > 7 PSI\" --> XV101[\"XV-101 Flush<br/>to Waste Header\"]\n        STR101 --> BF101[\"BF-101A/B Duplex 25 μm<br/>Bag Filters (HV-102)\"]\n        BF101 --> INJ_PACL[\"P-501 PACl Dosing<br/>(if > 5 NTU)\"]\n        INJ_PACL --> SM101[\"SM-101 316SS<br/>Static Mixer\"]\n        SM101 --> FIT200([\"FIT-200 & PT-200<br/>UF Feed Header\"])\n    end\n\n    subgraph S3[\"STAGE 3: PARALLEL HOLLOW-FIBER UF TRAINS A & B + DIRECT INTEGRITY TEST\"]\n        direction TB\n        subgraph UFA_ROW[\"UF Train A (125 GPM — 7x PVDF 0.02 μm Modules)\"]\n            direction LR\n            XV201A[\"XV-201A<br/>Train A Feed\"] --> UFA[\"UF-201A<br/>(7x PVDF Modules)\"]\n            UFA --> XV202A[\"XV-202A<br/>Train A Filtrate\"]\n            UFA -. \"BW / Drain\" .-> BW_A[\"XV-204A Upper &<br/>XV-205A Lower Drain\"]\n        end\n        subgraph UFB_ROW[\"UF Train B (125 GPM — 7x PVDF 0.02 μm Modules)\"]\n            direction LR\n            XV201B[\"XV-201B<br/>Train B Feed\"] --> UFB[\"UF-201B<br/>(7x PVDF Modules)\"]\n            UFB --> XV202B[\"XV-202B<br/>Train B Filtrate\"]\n            UFB -. \"BW / Drain\" .-> BW_B[\"XV-204B Upper &<br/>XV-205B Lower Drain\"]\n        end\n        subgraph UF_AUX[\"Shared UF Backwash, Air Scour & Daily Pressure-Decay DIT\"]\n            direction LR\n            P202[\"P-202 Backwash Pump (180 GPM)<br/>via XV-203A / XV-203B\"]\n            BL201[\"BL-201 Oil-Free Air Scour<br/>via XV-206A / XV-206B\"]\n            DIT[\"Regulated Instrument Air (XV-207A/B)<br/>+ PDIT-201A/B Pressure-Decay DIT\"]\n        end\n    end\n\n    P101 --> AIT101\n    FIT200 --> XV201A & XV201B\n    XV202A & XV202B --> TO_S4([\"To Stage 4: Combined UF Permeate Header (UF-3''-316SS)\"])\n```\n\n#### Sheet 1.2B — Stages 4, 5 & 6: UV, Chlorination, Contact Time, Clearwell & Triplex Booster\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph S45[\"STAGE 4 & 5: VALIDATED UV, NaOCl CHLORINATION & 3,000-GAL CONTACT TANK\"]\n        direction LR\n        FROM_S3([\"From Stage 3:<br/>UF Permeate\"]) --> AIT201([\"AIT-201 Turbidity<br/>(< 0.05 NTU)\"])\n        AIT201 -. \"> 0.15 NTU\" .-> XV300[\"XV-300 Off-Spec<br/>Divert to Waste\"]\n        AIT201 --> UV401[\"UV-401A/B/C Reactors<br/>(2 Duty + 1 Standby) + AIT-401 UVT\"]\n        UV401 --> INJ_CL2[\"P-502A/B 12.5%<br/>NaOCl Dosing + XV-401\"]\n        INJ_CL2 --> TK401[\"TK-401 3,000-Gal Baffled<br/>Contact Tank (LIT-401, T10 ≥ 6 min)\"]\n        TK401 --> AIT402([\"AIT-402 Free Cl2,<br/>pH & Temp (Live CT)\"])\n        AIT402 --> P401[\"P-401 CT Transfer<br/>Pump (5 HP VFD)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph S6[\"STAGE 6: ONBOARD CLEARWELL, TRIPLEX VFD BOOSTER & BUILDING TIE-IN\"]\n        direction LR\n        TK601[\"TK-601 500-Gal<br/>Baffled Clearwell (LIT-601)\"] --> AIT601([\"AIT-601 Finished<br/>Free Cl2 & pH\"])\n        TK601 -. \"BW Suction\" .-> P202_REF[\"To P-202 UF<br/>Backwash Pump\"]\n        TK601 -. \"Drain\" .-> XV601[\"XV-601 Clearwell<br/>Drain (3'')\"]\n        AIT601 --> P601[\"P-601A/B/C Triplex VFD Booster<br/>(2 Duty + 1 Standby, 15 HP ea)\"]\n        P601 --> PT602([\"PT-602 & FIT-602<br/>Discharge\"])\n        PT602 --> XV602[\"XV-602 Potable<br/>Isolation Valve\"]\n        XV602 --> RPZ602[\"RPZ-602 ASSE 1013<br/>Backflow Preventer\"]\n        RPZ602 --> OUT_A[\"Outlets A/B/C: Hospital, Restaurant<br/>& 12-Tap Manifold (PT-603 Remote)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph WASTE[\"COMMON DECHLORINATED WASTE HEADER (BW-3''-PVC80)\"]\n        direction LR\n        W_IN[\"All Flush, UF Backwash, Off-Spec<br/>Divert (XV-300) & Tank Drains\"] --> INJ_SBS[\"P-503 NaHSO3 Bisulfite<br/>Dechlorination Dosing\"]\n        INJ_SBS --> SEWER[\"Approved Sanitary Sewer Cleanout<br/>(0.0 mg/L Total Chlorine)\"]\n    end\n\n    P401 --> TK601\n    XV300 & XV601 --> W_IN\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Automated Valve Register (`XV` Series)\r\n\r\nAll automated valves are **316L stainless lug-style butterfly or ball valves** (NSF-61 EPDM seats) with rack-and-pinion pneumatic actuators (80 PSI instrument air, 24VDC Namur solenoids, open/closed limit switches to PLC inputs). **Instrument air is duplex:** two oil-free compressors (lead/lag) with a receiver sized to hold all valves in position for at least 10 minutes if both compressors stop. Low air pressure alarms at 65 PSI and starts the lag compressor.\r\n\r\n| Valve Tag | Size | Service / Description | Normal State | Fail Position (Air/Power Loss) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **`XV-100`** | 4.0\" | Raw Break Tank Fill (closes on low hydrant residual or high tank level) | **MODULATING / OPEN** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-101`** | 1.5\" | Auto-Strainer (`STR-101`) Blowdown | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-201A`** | 2.5\" | UF Train A Feed Inlet | **OPEN** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-202A`** | 2.5\" | UF Train A Permeate Outlet | **OPEN** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-203A`** | 2.5\" | UF Train A Backwash Supply | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-204A`** | 2.5\" | UF Train A Upper Backwash Reject / Vent | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-205A`** | 2.0\" | UF Train A Lower Drain | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-206A`** | 1.0\" | UF Train A Scour Air | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-207A`** | 0.5\" | UF Train A Integrity-Test Air | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-201B`** | 2.5\" | UF Train B Feed Inlet | **OPEN** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-202B`** | 2.5\" | UF Train B Permeate Outlet | **OPEN** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-203B`** | 2.5\" | UF Train B Backwash Supply | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-204B`** | 2.5\" | UF Train B Upper Backwash Reject / Vent | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-205B`** | 2.0\" | UF Train B Lower Drain | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-206B`** | 1.0\" | UF Train B Scour Air | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-207B`** | 0.5\" | UF Train B Integrity-Test Air | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-300`** | 3.0\" | Off-Spec UF Permeate Divert-to-Waste | **CLOSED** | **Fail-Open (FO)** |\r\n| **`XV-401`** | 3.0\" | Contact Tank `TK-401` Feed | **OPEN** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-601`** | 3.0\" | Clearwell Low-Point Drain | **CLOSED** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n| **`XV-602`** | 3.0\" | Final Potable Distribution Isolation | **OPEN** | Fail-Closed (FC) |\r\n\r\n**20 automated valves total.**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Automated PLC Valve & Pump Truth Matrix\r\n\r\nLegend:\r\n- **`O`** = Valve OPEN / **`C`** = Valve CLOSED / **`P`** = Pulsing (2 sec every 15 min or on ΔP) / **`M`** = Modulating on level\r\n- **`RUN`** = Pump/Unit Running / **`OFF`** = Stopped\r\n\r\n| Tag | Description | Mode 1: Startup Flush | Mode 1B: Fill & Prove | Mode 2: Normal (250 GPM) | Mode 3A: UF-A Backwash (45s) | Mode 3B: UF-B Backwash (45s) | Mode 4: Off-Spec Divert | Mode 5: Drain & Preserve | Mode 6: Daily DIT (Train A) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **`XV-100`** | Break Tank Fill | `M` | `M` | `M` | `M` | `M` | `M` | `C` | `M` |\r\n| **`XV-101`** | Strainer Blowdown | `P` | `P` | `P` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `O` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-201A`** | UF-A Feed | `O` | `O` | `O` | **`C`** | `O` | `O` | `C` | **`C`** |\r\n| **`XV-202A`** | UF-A Permeate | `O` | `O` | `O` | **`C`** | `O` | `O` | `C` | **`C`** |\r\n| **`XV-203A`** | UF-A BW Supply | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-204A`** | UF-A Top Drain | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** | `C` | `C` | `O` then `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-205A`** | UF-A Bottom Drain | `C` | `C` | `C` | `O` (last 10s) | `C` | `C` | `O` then `C` | **`O`** (drain feed side) |\r\n| **`XV-206A`** | UF-A Scour Air | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** (s 5–20) | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-207A`** | UF-A DIT Air | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** (pressurize, then hold) |\r\n| **`XV-201B`** | UF-B Feed | `O` | `O` | `O` | `O` | **`C`** | `O` | `C` | `O` |\r\n| **`XV-202B`** | UF-B Permeate | `O` | `O` | `O` | `O` | **`C`** | `O` | `C` | `O` |\r\n| **`XV-203B`** | UF-B BW Supply | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** | `C` | `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-204B`** | UF-B Top Drain | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** | `C` | `O` then `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-205B`** | UF-B Bottom Drain | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `O` (last 10s) | `C` | `O` then `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-206B`** | UF-B Scour Air | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** (s 5–20) | `C` | `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-207B`** | UF-B DIT Air | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-300`** | Off-Spec Divert | **`O`** | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** | `O` | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-401`** | Contact Tank Feed | `C` | **`O`** | **`O`** | **`O`** | **`O`** | **`C`** | `C` | `O` |\r\n| **`XV-601`** | Clearwell Drain | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | `C` | **`O`** | `C` |\r\n| **`XV-602`** | Potable Bldg Out | `C` | **`C`** | **`O`** | **`O`** | **`O`** | **`C`** | `C` | `O` |\r\n| **`P-101`** | Raw Intake VFD | `RUN` (150 GPM) | `RUN` | `RUN` (100%) | `RUN` (50%) | `RUN` (50%) | `RUN` | `OFF` | `RUN` (50%) |\r\n| **`P-202`** | UF Backwash Pump | `OFF` | `OFF` | `OFF` | **`RUN`** | **`RUN`** | `OFF` | `OFF` | `OFF` |\r\n| **`BL-201`** | Air Scour Blower | `OFF` | `OFF` | `OFF` | **`RUN`** | **`RUN`** | `OFF` | `OFF` | `OFF` |\r\n| **`P-401`** | CT Transfer Pump | `OFF` | `RUN` (on level) | `RUN` | `RUN` | `RUN` | `RUN` (until low) | `OFF` | `RUN` |\r\n| **`P-601`** | Triplex Bldg VFD | `OFF` | `OFF` | **`RUN`** | **`RUN`** | **`RUN`** | **`OFF`** | `OFF` | `RUN` |\r\n| **`P-503`** | Waste Dechlor | `RUN` | `OFF` | on waste flow | `RUN` | `RUN` | `RUN` | `RUN` | `OFF` |\r\n\r\n**Mode 1B (Fill & Prove)** is the step Rev A was missing. Permeate goes through UV and chlorination into the contact tank and clearwell, but `XV-602` stays closed until the PLC confirms: both UF trains passed today's DIT, permeate < 0.05 NTU for 3 minutes, UV dose ≥ 40 mJ/cm², and the contact tank has turned over at least once (≥ 3,000 gal through) with CT ≥ required. Only then does the HMI allow Mode 2.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Instrumentation & Fail-Safe Interlock Schedule\r\n\r\n> [!CAUTION]\r\n> **Potable Safety Interlocks:** Untreated or off-spec water must never reach the building outlets (`OUTLET A/B/C`). If any water-quality interlock trips, **`XV-602` closes** and the HMI shows the cause. `XV-300` fails open to waste on air or power loss.\r\n\r\n| Instrument Tag | Parameter Measured | Normal Operating Range | Alarm / Interlock Trip Point | Automated PLC Action on Trip |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **`PT-100`** | Hydrant Residual Pressure (upstream of `PSV-100`) | ≥ 20 PSI | **Low:** < 22 PSI / **Low-Low:** < 20 PSI | `PSV-100` holds 20 PSI mechanically; on Low-Low `XV-100` closes and HMI prompts **\"SWITCH TO TANKER SUPPLY.\"** The trailer never pulls the main below 20 PSI. |\r\n| **`LIT-100`** | Raw Break Tank Level | 40% to 90% | **High:** > 95% / **Low-Low:** < 15% | High closes `XV-100`; Low-Low stops `P-101` (dry-run protection). |\r\n| **`PSL/PT-101`** | `P-101` Suction Pressure | Positive head from tank | **Low-Low** (loss of prime) | Trips `P-101`. |\r\n| **`AIT-101`** | Raw Water Turbidity | 0.5 to 50 NTU | **High:** > 5.0 NTU | Starts PACl pump `P-501`, flow-paced. Above 50 NTU: alarm — source not suitable without upstream pretreatment. |\r\n| **`PDI-102`** | Auto-Strainer ΔP | 1.0 to 5.0 PSI | **High:** > 7.0 PSI | Opens `XV-101` and runs strainer brush for 3 seconds. |\r\n| **`PDIT-201A/B`** | UF Transmembrane Pressure | 4.0 to 18.0 PSI | **High:** > 22.0 PSI (or 45 min timer) | Starts the 45-second backwash on that train. |\r\n| **`PDIT-201A/B` (DIT)** | Pressure-Decay Rate (daily test) | Below maker's limit for the required log credit | **Fail:** decay above limit | Train held offline; HMI: **\"UF TRAIN INTEGRITY FAIL — PIN FIBERS / ISOLATE MODULE.\"** Plant continues at 125 GPM on the passing train. |\r\n| **`AIT-201`** | UF Permeate Turbidity | 0.015 to 0.050 NTU | **High-High:** > 0.15 NTU for 5 sec | Opens `XV-300` to waste, closes `XV-401` & `XV-602`, alerts operator of possible fiber breach. |\r\n| **`UIT-401A/B/C` + `AIT-401`** | UV Intensity & UVT | Validated dose ≥ 40 mJ/cm² | **Low-Low:** dose < 40 mJ/cm² | Starts standby reactor `UV-401C`; if dose is still low, cuts `P-101` flow to what the healthy reactors can treat; if still low, closes `XV-602`. |\r\n| **`AIT-402`** | Contact-Tank Outlet Free Cl₂, pH, Temp | Cl₂ 1.5–2.0 mg/L | **CT Low:** calculated CT < required for 4-log virus | Raises NaOCl dose; if CT is still short after one tank turnover, closes `XV-602`. |\r\n| **`LIT-401`** | Contact Tank Level | 80% to 95% | **Low:** < 70% | Low level shortens contact time — CT calculation uses actual volume; Low-Low stops `P-401`. |\r\n| **`LIT-601`** | Clearwell Level | 65% to 92% | **High:** > 95% / **Low-Low:** < 20% | PID trims `P-101`/`P-401` to hold 85%; trips `P-601` & `P-202` on Low-Low (dry-run protection). |\r\n| **`AIT-601`** | Finished Free Cl₂ & pH | Cl₂: 1.5–2.2 mg/L | **Low-Low:** < 0.8 mg/L or **High-High:** > 4.0 mg/L | Switches to standby NaOCl pump `P-502B`; if not corrected in 30 sec, closes `XV-602`. (4.0 mg/L is the EPA maximum residual disinfectant level.) |\r\n| **`PT-602` / `PT-603`** | Pump Discharge / Remote Building Pressure | Building 65 to 85 PSI | **High-High:** building > 90 PSI or pump > 110 PSI | VFDs control on `PT-603`; trip lead pump on High-High to protect building plumbing. |\r\n| **`PT-801`** | Instrument Air Header | 75 to 90 PSI | **Low:** < 65 PSI / **Low-Low:** < 55 PSI | Starts lag compressor; Low-Low alarms **\"AIR LOSS — VALVES WILL FAIL SAFE\"** (receiver gives ≥ 10 min warning). |\r\n| **`AIT-901`** | Chemical-Room Chlorine Gas | 0 ppm | **High:** ≥ 0.5 ppm | Exhaust fan to high, horn/strobe, stop chemical transfers, HMI alarm. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Automated Sequence Step-by-Step Descriptions\r\n\r\n### 5.1 UF Train Air-Scour Backwash Sequence (45 Seconds Total)\r\nEvery 45 minutes of runtime (or when TMP reaches 22 PSI), the PLC cleans one train while the other keeps supplying 125 GPM:\r\n1. **Step 1 — Isolate & Top-Vent (5 sec):** Close `XV-201A` and `XV-202A`. Open top drain `XV-204A`.\r\n2. **Step 2 — Air Scour (15 sec):** Start `BL-201`, open `XV-206A`.\r\n3. **Step 3 — Reverse Filtrate Flush (15 sec):** Close `XV-206A`. Open `XV-203A`, start `P-202` (180 GPM from the clearwell). Waste leaves through `XV-204A` into the common waste header, where `P-503` dechlorinates it.\r\n4. **Step 4 — Lower Sweep (10 sec):** Open `XV-205A` while `P-202` runs.\r\n5. **Step 5 — Return to Service:** Stop `P-202`, close `XV-203A`, `XV-204A`, `XV-205A`. Open `XV-201A` and `XV-202A`.\r\n\r\n### 5.2 Daily UF Direct Integrity Test (per train, about 10 minutes)\r\n1. Isolate the train (`XV-201A`, `XV-202A` closed) and drain the feed side through `XV-205A`.\r\n2. Open `XV-207A` to pressurize the feed side with regulated instrument air to the maker's test pressure, then close it.\r\n3. Hold and log the pressure decay on `PDIT-201A` for the maker's test period.\r\n4. Pass: refill and return to service. Fail: hold offline, alarm, record. Test results are logged for the state.\r\n\r\n### 5.3 One-Button \"Drain & Preserve for Transport\" Sequence (about 15 minutes)\r\nBefore retracting the jacks and towing, the operator presses **\"PREPARE FOR TRANSPORT\"** on the HMI:\r\n1. All water pumps (`P-101`, `P-202`, `P-401`, `P-601`) and chemical pumps lock out.\r\n2. Clearwell drain `XV-601` and strainer drain `XV-101` open; ground tanks `TK-100` and `TK-401` are drained to sewer through the waste header (with dechlorination) and folded by the crew.\r\n3. UF feed sides drain through `XV-205A/B`, then close.\r\n4. **Preservation fill:** The CIP pump fills both UF trains with membrane preservative from the CIP tank (sodium bisulfite solution; glycol-based preservative when freezing weather is possible), then all UF valves close. **Membranes are never blown dry.**\r\n5. Once `LIT-601` reads 0% and the preservation step logs complete, the HMI shows **\"DRAINED & PRESERVED — VERIFY ROAD WEIGHT ON SCALE BEFORE TOWING.\"**\r\n","MWTP_250_BOM_and_Cost_Breakdown.md":"# MWTP-250 Bill of Materials (BOM) & Capital Cost Breakdown\r\n\r\n**Revision B — November 2026.** Reflects the Rev B design: break tank and hydrant kit, contact tank and transfer pump, UF integrity testing, third UV reactor, larger pumps and generator, duplex instrument air, segregated chemical room and safety station; onboard GAC removed. Adds engineering, and a 15% contingency for a first-of-a-kind build.\r\n\r\n## 1. Executive Cost Summary\r\n\r\nThese are **budget estimates** for one **MWTP-250 (250 GPM rated)** unit in a custom 36-ft triple-axle gooseneck. Final pricing is confirmed by written vendor quotes.\r\n\r\n| Category | Subsystem Description | Equipment Cost (USD) | % of Hardware |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **1.0** | **36-ft Triple-Axle Gooseneck, 9.5-ft Tall Box, Firewall & Hydraulic Outriggers** | $64,500 | 14.9% |\r\n| **2.0** | **Stage 1 & 2: Hydrant Kit, Raw Break Tank, 15 HP Intake Pump, Auto-Strainer & Bag Filters** | $34,200 | 7.9% |\r\n| **3.0** | **Stage 3: Hollow-Fiber UF Skid (14 Modules) + Direct Integrity Test** | $80,200 | 18.5% |\r\n| **4.0** | **Stage 4 & 5: 3 UV Reactors, Chemical Dosing, Segregated Chemical Room & Contact Tank** | $59,800 | 13.8% |\r\n| **5.0** | **Stage 6: 500-Gal Clearwell, Triplex 15 HP VFD Booster & RPZ Backflow** | $42,700 | 9.9% |\r\n| **6.0** | **316L SS Process Piping, 20 Pneumatic Valves (`XV`), Bulkhead & Hose Kits** | $43,800 | 10.1% |\r\n| **7.0** | **PLC/SCADA, Analyzers, Wet Lab & Safety Station** | $46,800 | 10.8% |\r\n| **8.0** | **80 kW Tier-4 Diesel Generator, 150-Gal Fuel, ATS & Duplex Instrument Air** | $61,000 | 14.1% |\r\n| — | **SUBTOTAL — MAJOR EQUIPMENT & MATERIALS** | **$433,000** | **100.0%** |\r\n| **9.0** | **System Integration, 316L SS TIG Welding, UL-508A Panel Wiring & FAT Testing** | $68,000 | — |\r\n| **10.0** | **Engineering: PE Basis-of-Design, Stamped Drawings, State Plan Review** | $22,000 | — |\r\n| **11.0** | **Contingency (15% of items 1.0–10.0, first-of-a-kind build)** | $78,450 | — |\r\n| — | **TOTAL TURNKEY BUILD COST (Single Unit)** | **$601,450** | — |\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> **Commercial comparison:** Get at least two written quotes for a comparable turnkey mobile UF plant before using any OEM price comparison with a lender or customer. Rev A's \"$650,000–$850,000 OEM retail\" figure has no quote behind it and has been removed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Line-Item Bill of Materials (BOM)\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Category 1.0: Mobility Platform & Enclosure (`$64,500`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **1.1** | 1 | **36-ft Triple-Axle Commercial Gooseneck** (28 ft main deck + 8 ft gooseneck), **9.5-ft main-deck interior** within 13'-6\" legal height, 3 × 10,000 lb torsion axles, electric-over-hydraulic disc brakes, 30,000 lb GVWR | ATC / Featherlite / Custom Commercial | $44,000 | $44,000 |\r\n| **1.2** | 1 | **Insulated Package + Machinery Compartment:** R-12 closed-cell foam, aluminum treadplate floor with perimeter drains, **fire-separated gooseneck generator compartment with exterior doors and radiator/exhaust venting**, rear barn doors + side doors, curbside belly boxes | Factory Upfit | $8,500 | $8,500 |\r\n| **1.3** | 1 | **4-Point Hydraulic Auto-Leveling Outrigger Jacks** | Equalizer Systems / Bulldog | $6,400 | $6,400 |\r\n| **1.4** | 1 | **24,000 BTU Heat Pump HVAC + Unit Heaters + Chemical-Room Exhaust Fan** | Mitsubishi / Qmark | $5,600 | $5,600 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Category 2.0: Supply, Break Tank & Pre-Filtration (`$34,200`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **2.1** | 1 | **Raw Water Intake Pump (`P-101`):** Self-priming, 260 GPM @ 130 ft TDH, **15 HP** TEFC, 316SS wet end, VFD | Gorman-Rupp / Goulds / Grundfos | $7,200 | $7,200 |\r\n| **2.2** | 1 | **Automatic Self-Cleaning Strainer (`STR-101`):** 3\" 316SS, 200 μm wedge-wire, motorized scanner with ΔP switch (`PDI-102`) | Eaton / Amiad SAF-1500 | $9,800 | $9,800 |\r\n| **2.3** | 1 | **316L SS Inline Static Mixer (`SM-101`):** 3\", single injection quill (PACl), installed **after** the bag filters | Koflo 3\" 316SS | $1,400 | $1,400 |\r\n| **2.4** | 1 | **Duplex Bag Filter Housing (`BF-101A/B`):** 2 × 4-bag 304SS with transfer valve + 100 spare 25 μm bags | Eaton Duoline / Pentair | $8,200 | $8,200 |\r\n| **2.5** | 1 | **Raw-Water Break Tank (`TK-100`):** 3,000-gal open-top frame tank, NSF-61 liner, cover, air-gap fill nozzle, level transmitter (`LIT-100`) | Husky / Fol-Da-Tank | $5,200 | $5,200 |\r\n| **2.6** | 1 | **Hydrant Kit:** Pressure-sustaining valve `PSV-100` (set 20 PSI), `PT-100`, fill valve `XV-100` actuator package, hydrant wrench. *Hydrant meter and backflow device are normally rented from the utility with the hydrant permit.* | Cla-Val / Watts | $2,400 | $2,400 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Category 3.0: Hollow-Fiber Ultrafiltration Skid (`$80,200`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **3.1** | 14 | **PVDF Hollow-Fiber UF Modules (`UF-201A/B`):** 0.02 μm, 775 ft² (72 m²) each, **NSF/ANSI 61 + NSF/ANSI 419 challenge-tested** | DuPont IntegraFlux / Toray / Veolia | $2,650 | $37,100 |\r\n| **3.2** | 1 | **UF Skid Frame & Manifolds:** Dual-train rack (2 × 7) with vibration mounts — height to suit 9.5-ft ceiling | Custom Fabricated | $14,500 | $14,500 |\r\n| **3.3** | 1 | **UF Backwash Pump (`P-202`):** 180 GPM @ 40 PSI, 5.0 HP 316SS, soft-starter | Grundfos / Ebara 316SS | $3,600 | $3,600 |\r\n| **3.4** | 1 | **Air Scour Blower (`BL-201`) & CIP Tank (`TK-203`):** 2.0 HP oil-free blower + 100-gal CIP tank with 6 kW heater and CIP pump | Gast / Fuji + Custom CIP | $6,800 | $6,800 |\r\n| **3.5** | 1 | **UF Local Valve & Sensor Harness:** sanitary couplings, sample valves, air vents, gauges | Integrated Package | $12,200 | $12,200 |\r\n| **3.6** | 1 | **Direct Integrity Test Panel (`DIT-201`):** regulated air supply, high-resolution pressure transmitters, PLC test routine | Membrane OEM package | $4,800 | $4,800 |\r\n| **3.7** | 1 | **Membrane Preservation Kit:** preservative chemical stock, glycol for freeze season, fill fittings | Membrane OEM recommended | $1,200 | $1,200 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2.4 Category 4.0: UV, Chemical Dosing, Chemical Room & Contact Tank (`$59,800`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **4.1** | 3 | **Validated Amalgam UV Reactors (`UV-401A/B/C`):** 125 GPM each, **2 duty + 1 standby**, 316L SS, ≥ 40 mJ/cm² **EPA UVDGM validated**, intensity sensors & controller | TrojanUV / Xylem Wedeco / Evoqua | $9,600 | $28,800 |\r\n| **4.2** | 1 | **Online UVT Analyzer (`AIT-401`)** | Real Tech / Hach | $4,500 | $4,500 |\r\n| **4.3** | 5 | **Digital Metering Pumps:** `P-501` PACl, `P-502A/B` NaOCl duty/standby, `P-503` bisulfite (waste dechlor), `P-504` citric acid (CIP) | Grundfos DDA / ProMinent | $2,200 | $11,000 |\r\n| **4.4** | Lot | **Segregated Chemical Storage:** separate oxidizer, acid/reducer and PACl cabinets with individual spill containment; small-container (≤ 8 gal) stock for transport | Justrite / Denios | — | $5,500 |\r\n| **4.5** | 1 | **Baffled Contact Tank (`TK-401`):** 3,000-gal NSF-61 frame tank with internal baffle curtains, cover, level transmitter (`LIT-401`) | Husky / SEI (custom baffles) | $6,800 | $6,800 |\r\n| **4.6** | 1 | **Contact Tank Transfer Pump (`P-401`):** self-priming 260 GPM @ 40 ft, 5 HP, VFD | Gorman-Rupp / Grundfos | $3,200 | $3,200 |\r\n\r\n*Removed from Rev A: 2 × 36\" GAC vessels ($10,400) and 50 ft³ GAC media ($5,500).*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2.5 Category 5.0: Clearwell, Building Booster Skid & Backflow (`$42,700`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **5.1** | 1 | **500-Gal Baffled Clearwell (`TK-601`):** NSF-61 XLPE rectangular tank (~32\" × 48\" × 75\") with baffles and steel transport cage | Custom Plastic Fab | $4,900 | $4,900 |\r\n| **5.2** | 1 | **Triplex VFD Booster Skid (`P-601A/B/C`):** 3 × 125 GPM @ ~100 PSI, **15 HP each (2 duty + 1 standby)**, 316SS, individual VFDs, 85-gal ASME bladder tank, remote pressure transmitter (`PT-603`) | Grundfos Hydro MPC-E / Goulds | $32,000 | $32,000 |\r\n| **5.3** | 1 | **3\" RPZ Backflow Preventer (`RPZ-602`):** lead-free, ASSE 1013 / AWWA, field-testable | Watts 957 / Wilkins 375XL | $5,800 | $5,800 |\r\n\r\n*Rev A's 3,000-gal pillow bladder is replaced by the baffled contact tank in item 4.5.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2.6 Category 6.0: Piping, Pneumatic Valves & Hose Kits (`$43,800`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **6.1** | 20 | **Pneumatically Actuated 316SS Valves (`XV-100` to `XV-602`):** 0.5\" to 4\" NSF-61 seats, spring-return actuators, 24VDC solenoids & limit switches | Bray / Keystone | $850 avg | $17,000 |\r\n| **6.2** | Lot | **Schedule 10 316L SS Piping, Check Valves & Manual Valves:** headers, hangers, heat tracing | Victaulic / Swagelok | — | $13,800 |\r\n| **6.3** | 1 | **Bulkhead, 12-Tap Manifold & Hose Kits:** 100 ft 4\" NSF-61 hydrant-to-tank hose, 2 × 50 ft 4\" contact-tank hoses, 150 ft 3\" NSF-61 potable hose, 200 ft 3\" waste hose, hose ramps, **hose-sanitizing kit** (batch tank, pump, test strips) | Snap-Tite / Kuriyama NSF-61 | $13,000 | $13,000 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2.7 Category 7.0: PLC SCADA, Online Water Quality Lab & Safety (`$46,800`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **7.1** | 1 | **Main PLC Control Cabinet (`CP-701`):** NEMA 4X, CompactLogix 5380, 15\" PanelView Plus, UPS, Starlink/cellular gateway, CT calculation logic | Rockwell Automation | $14,200 | $14,200 |\r\n| **7.2** | 2 | **Laser Turbidity Analyzers (`AIT-101`, `AIT-201`)** | Hach TU5300sc + SC200 | $5,400 | $10,800 |\r\n| **7.3** | 2 | **Free Chlorine, pH & Temperature Analyzer Panels (`AIT-402` contact-tank outlet, `AIT-601` finished)** | Hach CLF10sc + pHD | $5,800 | $11,600 |\r\n| **7.4** | Lot | **Mag Meters, Pressure Transmitters, Level Transmitters, DR900 Colorimeter, Bench Turbidimeter, Bac-T Sampling Supplies** | Endress+Hauser / IFM / Hach | — | $5,400 |\r\n| **7.5** | Lot | **Safety Station:** ANSI Z358.1 tempered eyewash/drench, chlorine gas detector (`AIT-901`), spill kits, fire extinguishers, scene lighting, traffic cones & signs | Bradley / Honeywell / Justrite | — | $4,800 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2.8 Category 8.0: Power & Instrument Air (`$61,000`)\r\n\r\n| Item | Qty | Description & Technical Specification | Reference OEM / Model | Unit Cost | Extended |\r\n| :--- | :-: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **8.1** | 1 | **80 kW Prime Tier-4 Final Diesel Generator:** sound-attenuated, 208/480V 3-phase, 150-gal sub-base fuel tank, sized for VFD loads | Cummins / Caterpillar / Kohler | $48,000 | $48,000 |\r\n| **8.2** | 1 | **200A ATS & Shore Power Camlock Panel (480V)** with shore cable | ASCO 300 / Hubbell | $6,800 | $6,800 |\r\n| **8.3** | 1 | **Duplex Oil-Free Scroll Compressors (2 × 2 HP, lead/lag) + Receiver + Refrigerated Dryer (`AC-802A/B`)** | Atlas Copco SF2 / Quincy | $6,200 | $6,200 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Operating Cost (OpEx) per Day of Deployment\r\n\r\nAt **250 GPM rated (~350,000 gallons/day net)** on generator power. Crew labor is in the business plan, not here.\r\n\r\n| Consumable / Operating Item | Consumption Rate | Unit Cost | Daily Cost | Cost per 1,000 Gal (net) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **Off-Road Diesel Fuel** (37.8 kW avg load) | ~3.1 gal/hr (74 gal/day) | $3.80 / gal | $281.20 | $0.80 |\r\n| **12.5% Sodium Hypochlorite** (~2.5 mg/L dose for 1.5–2.0 mg/L residual) | 7.2 gal/day | $3.25 / gal | $23.40 | $0.07 |\r\n| **PACl Coagulant** (only if raw turbidity > 5 NTU) | 4.50 gal/day | $4.50 / gal | $20.25 | $0.06 |\r\n| **Bag Filters & CIP Chemicals** (amortized) | 2 bags/day avg | $6.50 / bag | $13.00 | $0.04 |\r\n| **Bac-T Lab Samples & Field Reagents** | Per state sampling plan | — | $75.00 | $0.21 |\r\n| **Membrane, UV Lamp & Consumable Reserve** | — | — | $38.00 | $0.11 |\r\n| **TOTAL OPERATING COST (Generator Power)** | — | — | **$450.85 / day** | **$1.29 / 1,000 Gal** |\r\n| **TOTAL OPERATING COST (Shore Power, ~907 kWh/day at $0.13/kWh)** | — | — | **$287.59 / day** | **$0.82 / 1,000 Gal** |\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> At about $1.29 per 1,000 gallons in consumables, the plant's water costs roughly a tenth of a cent per gallon before labor. Delivered bulk or bottled water typically costs far more per gallon and doesn't keep a building pressurized — but quote local tanker and bottled-water prices before putting a specific comparison in front of a customer.\r\n","MWTP_250_Hospital_Restaurant_Tie_In_SOP.md":"# MWTP-250 & MWTP-50 Field Standard Operating Procedure (SOP): Hospital & Restaurant Emergency Water Tie-In\r\n\r\n**Document ID:** `SOP-MWTP-FIELD-01` — **Revision B (November 2026)**  \r\n**Applicable Fleet Units:**\r\n* **`MWTP-250` (Hospital Flagship):** 36-ft Triple-Axle Gooseneck (`30,000 lb GVWR`, 250 GPM rated, 80 kW generator, towed by Class 5 truck — **Class A CDL required**).\r\n* **`MWTP-50-GN22` (\"KitchenShield-50\" Restaurant Unit):** 22-ft Tandem-Axle Compact Gooseneck (`16-ft` main deck + `6-ft` gooseneck deck, **`9,990 lb GVWR`**, 35 GPM continuous / 50 GPM peak booster, 20 kW generator or 240V 50A shore power, towed by 1-ton SRW pickup — **No Class A CDL required**; standard driver's license + DOT medical card).\r\n\r\n**References:** CDC/AWWA *Emergency Water Supply Planning Guide for Hospitals and Healthcare Facilities*, ASSE 1013 (RPZ backflow prevention), NSF/ANSI 61, the state drinking water program's emergency-use approval for these units, OSHA 29 CFR 1910 (HazCom, PPE, eyewash 1910.151(c)), MUTCD (temporary traffic control).\r\n\r\n> [!IMPORTANT]\r\n> **Before any deployment:** the site must be **pre-surveyed** (connection point, valve locations, hose route, downstream sanitary sewer cleanout), the **state emergency-use approval** must be in hand, and the crew must include a **state-certified water treatment operator** and a qualified driver (**Class A CDL** for the `MWTP-250`; **Standard License + DOT Medical Card** for the `MWTP-50-GN22`). Utility staff operate utility valves (curb stops, meter valves, hydrants) unless the utility authorizes us in writing.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Golden Rules of Building Pressurization\r\n\r\n> [!CAUTION]\r\n> **RULE 1 — ISOLATE THE BUILDING FROM THE STREET MAIN FIRST:** Before pumping treated water into any building, the building's service valve (curb stop / OS&Y gate valve at the meter vault) must be closed — by the utility or the facility's engineer with the utility's OK. If it's left open, treated water at 70 PSI flows backward into the depressurized city main, draining the trailer and mixing with unsafe water.\r\n>\r\n> **RULE 2 — NEVER TIE INTO A FIRE SPRINKLER FDC:** A building's Fire Department Connection (`FDC`) feeds stagnant fire sprinkler piping, **not** the domestic potable plumbing. Only use the **domestic water tie-in** identified in the site survey.\r\n>\r\n> **RULE 3 — NEVER PULL THE MAIN BELOW 20 PSI:** Neither trailer ever pumps directly from a hydrant or street service line. Raw water goes through the pressure-sustaining valve (`PSV-100`, set at 20 PSI) into the raw break tank (`TK-100`) through an air gap. If the hydrant or upstream tap can't hold 20 PSI residual, stop and switch to tanker supply.\r\n>\r\n> **RULE 4 — NEVER DISCHARGE BACKWASH THROUGH A RESTAURANT GREASE TRAP:** On restaurant deployments (`MWTP-50`), never connect the gray backwash/flush hose (`35–60 GPM`) to a fixture upstream of the kitchen's F.O.G. grease interceptor. High-flow flush water will wash trapped grease into the city sewer. Always discharge to the **downstream sanitary sewer cleanout** (street side of the grease trap) mapped during the AmeriPro site survey.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Site Setup & Water Supply (0:00–1:15)\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Positioning, Traffic Control & Stabilization\r\n1. **Traffic control:** Set cones and signs per MUTCD before anyone works near the roadway or drive-thru lane. Use hose ramps wherever hoses cross traffic or walkways.\r\n2. **Site selection:**\r\n   * **`MWTP-250` (Hospital):** Park on firm pavement with room beside the trailer for two 3,000-gal ground tanks (about `12 ft × 12 ft` each), within **100 ft** of the hydrant/tanker access, within **150 ft** of the building tie-in, and within **200 ft** of the approved sanitary sewer cleanout.\r\n   * **`MWTP-50-GN22` (Restaurant):** Use the 22-ft gooseneck's tight turning radius to back into the pre-surveyed 2-parking-space service bay next to the kitchen utility room, leaving room beside the trailer for the 550-gal raw break tank (`5 ft × 5 ft`) and 600-gal contact tank (`5.5 ft × 5.5 ft`).\r\n3. **Deploy stabilizer jacks / outriggers:**\r\n   * Keep the truck hitched or chock all trailer tires.\r\n   * Engage the **4-point leveling jacks** (`Control Panel Switch -> AUTO LEVEL` on `MWTP-250`, or 4 corner electric/hydraulic stabilizer jacks + gooseneck landing gear on `MWTP-50`) and verify all four pads are firmly planted before filling the onboard clearwell.\r\n4. **Power up:**\r\n   * **`MWTP-250` Generator / Shore:** Open the gooseneck compartment's exterior louvers and doors, start the **80 kW generator**, and verify 480V/208V 3-phase power at `CP-701`. (Optional shore power only from a hospital or utility source approved in writing; open-transition ATS).\r\n   * **`MWTP-50-GN22` Shore Plug or 20 kW Generator:**\r\n     * *Boil Water Advisory (Grid Power On):* Connect the 50-ft **240V 50A single-phase twist-lock cord** to the restaurant's pre-installed 50A exterior receptacle and select **`[50A SHORE MODE]`** on `CP-701` (caps draw at 40A / $0 diesel burn).\r\n     * *Power Outage (or No Shore Plug):* Open the gooseneck deck louvers and start the **20 kW Tier-4 diesel generator** (`240V/120V` single-phase). Keep the crew out of the gooseneck generator bay while running.\r\n5. **Chemical compartment check:** Confirm the chlorine gas detector (`AIT-901`) reads 0 ppm, the exhaust fan runs, and the ANSI Z358.1 eyewash/drench hose has water. Wear chemical goggles, face shield, and gloves for any chemical handling. Bulk bleach bought locally stays in its containers and is never stored next to acids.\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Deploy the Ground Tanks\r\n1. Set up **`TK-100` (raw break tank — blue tag)** and **`TK-401` (baffled contact tank — white tag)** on level ground, covers on (`3,000 gal` each on `MWTP-250`; `550 gal` and `600 gal` on `MWTP-50`). Never swap their hoses: raw water hoses are blue, treated water hoses are white.\r\n2. Connect `TK-100` outlet to **Trailer `RAW INLET A`** (`4\"` suction on `MWTP-250`; `2\"` suction on `MWTP-50`).\r\n3. Connect **`CT OUT`** and **`CT RETURN`** to `TK-401`.\r\n4. Connect the **waste hose** (`3\"` on `MWTP-250`; `2\"` on `MWTP-50`) from `WASTE OUTLET` to the approved **downstream sanitary sewer cleanout** (downstream of any grease trap). Storm drains only with written MS4/state permission.\r\n\r\n### 2.3A Hydrant Supply (when the main still has pressure — `MWTP-250` or `MWTP-50`)\r\n1. **Utility permission:** The hydrant permit, hydrant meter, and backflow device come from the utility. Fire flow has priority: if the fire department needs the hydrant, release it.\r\n2. **Flush the hydrant** (utility or with utility permission): open slowly for **60–120 seconds** to clear rust and sediment, then close.\r\n3. **Install hydrant hardware in this order:** hydrant → utility meter + backflow → `PT-100` / **`PSV-100` pressure-sustaining valve (set 20 PSI)** → `XV-100` fill valve → blue raw hose → **air-gap fill nozzle on `TK-100`**. The fill nozzle stays at least two pipe diameters above the tank's overflow rim.\r\n4. **Open slowly** and confirm on the HMI that hydrant residual (`PT-100`) holds **≥ 20 PSI** while `TK-100` fills. If it won't, close the hydrant and go to §2.4.\r\n\r\n### 2.3B Restaurant Upstream Service Tap (Boil Water Advisory — `MWTP-50` Only)\r\nWhen deploying the **`MWTP-50`** to a restaurant during a Boil Water Advisory where the city main has pressure (`40–70 PSI`) and no fire hydrant is immediately adjacent:\r\n1. Close the restaurant's building isolation valves and confirm **double isolation** between raw and treated water: either **two closed valves** in series, or one valve leak-tested closed (open a downstream drain and confirm no flow for 2 minutes). Tag both.\r\n2. Locate the **pre-installed 1.5\" valved service-line tee** that the restaurant's licensed plumber installed during the site survey, between the meter and the building isolation valves, with the utility's approval. Never draw from a hose bibb or a backflow test cock — test cocks can't pass 35 GPM, and most bibbs sit downstream of the building shutoff.\r\n3. Connect a 1.5\" blue raw-water hose from the tee through the trailer's **`HYDRANT / TAP INLET`** (`PT-100` + **`PSV-100` set at 20 PSI** + `XV-100`) into the **air-gap fill nozzle on the 550-gal break tank (`TK-100`)**.\r\n4. Verify on the HMI that upstream street pressure (`PT-100`) holds **≥ 20 PSI** at 15–35 GPM.\r\n\r\n### 2.4 Tanker Supply (water outage, or main can't hold 20 PSI)\r\n1. Tankers must be potable-water haulers per the state's approval. Record each tanker's source and chlorine residual.\r\n2. Tankers discharge into `TK-100` through the air-gap fill:\r\n   * **`MWTP-250` (Hospital at 250 GPM):** Uses up to **15,000 gal/hr** — requires a scheduled multi-tanker shuttle or reduced flow rate.\r\n   * **`MWTP-50` (Restaurant at 4,000–8,000 gal/day):** A **single 4,000-gallon potable tanker delivery** (or 1–2 drops/day) sustains a commercial kitchen for an entire operating day.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Identifying the Building Tie-In Point (Hospital vs. Restaurant)\r\n\r\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph City[\"1. Municipal Street Main & Source\"]\n        direction LR\n        CM[\"Street Water Main\"] --> HYD[\"Fire Hydrant OR<br/>Upstream Service-Line Tap\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Supply[\"2. Raw Supply Protection & Air-Gap Break Tank\"]\n        direction LR\n        PSV[\"Utility Meter / Backflow +<br/>PSV-100 (hold ≥ 20 PSI)\"] --> TK100[\"TK-100 Raw Break Tank<br/>(Air Gap: 3,000 or 550 Gal)\"]\n        TANKER[\"Potable Water Tanker<br/>(if main has zero pressure)\"] --> TK100\n    end\n\n    subgraph Trailer[\"3. MWTP-250 / MWTP-50 / MWTP-50C Treatment & Booster\"]\n        direction LR\n        TREAT[\"UF (0.02 μm + DIT)<br/>+ Redundant UV\"] --> CT[\"4-Log Virus Barrier:<br/>TK-401 CT Tank or 186 mJ/cm² UV\"]\n        CT --> BOOST[\"Onboard Clearwell +<br/>VFD Booster + RPZ-602\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Facility[\"4. Hospital / Restaurant Domestic Entry\"]\n        direction LR\n        V_ISO[\"1. Building Service Valve<br/>CLOSED & TAGGED\"] --> PORT[\"2. Pre-Installed Domestic<br/>Tie-In (NEVER the FDC)\"]\n        PORT --> DIST[\"Pressurized Building<br/>Plumbing (60–85 PSI)\"]\n    end\n\n    HYD == \"Blue Raw Hose\" ==> PSV\n    CM -. \"X ISOLATED X\" .-> V_ISO\n    TK100 == \"4'' or 2'' Suction\" ==> TREAT\n    BOOST == \"3'' or 2'' Sanitized White Potable Hose\" ==> PORT\n```\r\n\r\n### Option A: Acute-Care Hospital Tie-In (`MWTP-250` — 100 to 250 GPM)\r\nEvery hospital under a retainer has a **pre-installed emergency domestic water connection** designed during the site survey, installed by the hospital's licensed plumber, and approved by the utility (typically a flanged or camlock connection on the building side of the meter/backflow assembly, labeled *\"EMERGENCY DOMESTIC WATER INLET — POTABLE ONLY\"*).\r\n1. Locate the emergency potable connection identified in the site survey.\r\n2. The utility or hospital engineer closes the street isolation valve upstream of the hospital's meter/backflow assembly and tags it.\r\n3. Connect the **3\" white NSF-61 potable hose** from **`MWTP-250 OUTLET A`** to the emergency inlet (after sanitizing — §4.3).\r\n4. **Coordinate with the hospital's water management program team and infection prevention** before re-pressurizing. Stagnant hospital plumbing is a Legionella risk; the hospital decides its own flushing and testing plan.\r\n\r\n**No pre-installed connection?** Do not remove meter spools, meter bypass pieces, or strainer bonnets yourself. Those belong to the utility and the facility. Stop and arrange a temporary connection with the utility and the facility's licensed plumber.\r\n\r\n### Option B: Restaurant / Commercial Kitchen Tie-In (`MWTP-50-GN22` — 15 to 50 GPM)\r\n1. The connection point and adapter sizes come from the AmeriPro site survey (typically a 1.5\" or 2\" camlock tee with isolation valve on the **building side** of the meter or backflow assembly, installed by the restaurant's licensed plumber).\r\n2. The utility (or the owner/plumber, with the utility's OK) closes the curb stop or main shutoff upstream of the tie-in tee. (If using §2.3B upstream BWA tap, confirm the closed valve is *between* the upstream raw tap and the downstream potable tie-in tee!).\r\n3. Connect the **1.5\" or 2\" white NSF-61 potable hose** from **`MWTP-50 POTABLE OUTLET A`** after sanitizing (§4.3).\r\n4. Confirm the trailer's water feeds **upstream** of ice machines, beverage systems, water heaters, and dish machines, and that the restaurant's health inspector has accepted the arrangement as an approved alternate source.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Start-Up, Proving & Hose Sanitization (1:15–2:00)\r\n\r\n### 4.1 Mode 1 — Startup Flush\r\n1. On the HMI (`CP-701`), press **`[AUTO STARTUP & FLUSH]`**.\r\n2. The PLC runs `P-101` (`150 GPM` on `MWTP-250`; `25 GPM` on `MWTP-50`) through the strainer, bags, and UF, with permeate going **to waste** through `XV-300` (dechlorinated by `P-503`). `XV-401` and `XV-602` stay closed.\r\n3. Continue until UF permeate turbidity (`AIT-201`) reads **< 0.05 NTU for 3 minutes**.\r\n\r\n### 4.2 Integrity Test & Mode 1B — Fill & Prove\r\n1. **Direct integrity test:** If today's test isn't logged, press **`[RUN DIT]`**. Each train must pass the pressure-decay test before its water can count. A failed train stays offline.\r\n2. Press **`[FILL & PROVE]`**. Water now passes through UV and chlorination into the contact tank `TK-401` and the onboard clearwell `TK-601`. `XV-602` stays closed.\r\n3. The HMI releases the building outlet only when all of these are green:\r\n   * UF permeate turbidity **< 0.05 NTU** (trip at 0.15 NTU)\r\n   * UV dose **≥ 40 mJ/cm²** on the duty reactor(s)\r\n   * Contact tank turned over at least once (**≥ 3,000 gal** on `MWTP-250`; **≥ 600 gal** on `MWTP-50`)\r\n   * **CT ≥ required** for 4-log virus at the measured temperature and pH (`AIT-402`)\r\n   * Finished free chlorine **1.5–2.2 mg/L** (`AIT-601`)\r\n4. **Bac-T samples:** Pull total coliform samples at the clearwell outlet sample tap, label them, and send to the state-certified lab with the chain-of-custody form. Follow the state's emergency approval for whether water can be used before results return (often 18–24 hours); until the state says otherwise, the facility's boil-water precautions stay in place.\r\n\r\n### 4.3 Sanitize the Potable Delivery Hose (separate from the plant)\r\nHoses are sanitized with the **hose-sanitizing kit**, not by raising the plant's chlorine setpoint (which would trip the 4.0 mg/L high-high interlock).\r\n1. Batch **50 mg/L** chlorine solution in the kit tank (from the oxidizer cabinet, using the dilution chart on the kit).\r\n2. Fill the potable hose (`3\"` on `MWTP-250`; `1.5\"` or `2\"` on `MWTP-50`) completely, cap both ends, and hold for **30 minutes**.\r\n3. Drain the solution through the waste header (dechlorinated).\r\n4. Connect the hose to `OUTLET A` with the building end open to a drain bucket, open `XV-602` from the HMI, and flush with finished water (`50 GPM` on `MWTP-250`; `25 GPM` on `MWTP-50`) until the free chlorine at the hose end (Hach pocket colorimeter) matches the trailer's finished residual and the water is clear (bench turbidimeter).\r\n5. Close `XV-602`, spray the building connection and hose coupling with **200 ppm** chlorine solution, and lock the hose onto the building tie-in.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Slow Building Pressurization & Air Venting (2:00–2:30)\r\n\r\n> [!WARNING]\r\n> **Water hammer prevention:** If the building's pipes drained during the outage, slamming full pressure into empty pipes can burst joints or damage kitchen solenoids and hospital sterilizers. Always use the **2-stage soft-ramp**.\r\n\r\n1. **Stage 1 — Low-pressure fill (30 PSI setpoint):**\r\n   * On the HMI, set the booster setpoint (`PT-603`, building side) to **30 PSI** and enable **`[SOFT RAMP FILL]`**.\r\n   * Open the building inlet valve slowly over **60 seconds** (quarter-turn every 15 seconds).\r\n   * Facility staff open faucets on the **highest floor** (or the farthest 3-comp prep sink in a restaurant) to bleed air until water flows steadily.\r\n2. **Stage 2 — Normal operating pressure:**\r\n   * **1–2 story restaurant / clinic (`MWTP-50`):** **60 to 70 PSI**\r\n   * **3–6 story hospital (`MWTP-250`):** **75 to 85 PSI** at the building (the hospital's own booster pumps handle taller buildings).\r\n3. **Leak & backflow check:** Inspect camlock gaskets, the trailer's `RPZ-602` relief port, and the closed street valve. Confirm stable pressure.\r\n4. **Point-of-use check:** At the **farthest faucet / prep sink**, record **free chlorine (≥ 1.0 mg/L)**, **pH (7.0–8.0)**, and **turbidity (< 0.10 NTU)** in the log, and pull a Bac-T sample there too.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Field Operator Quick-Reference Checklist\r\n\r\n| Step | Action Item | Verified By / Target Metric (`MWTP-250` / `MWTP-50`) |\r\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **1** | State approval in hand; certified operator + qualified driver (Class A CDL for `250`, Std + Med Card for `50`) | Paperwork in trailer binder |\r\n| **2** | Traffic control set; trailer level on 4 jacks; ground tanks deployed (`3,000 gal` or `550/600 gal`) | Cones/signs up; bubble level centered |\r\n| **3** | Generator running (or 240V 50A shore plug on `MWTP-50`); air at 80 PSI; chem bay 0 ppm Cl₂ | Power normal at `CP-701`; `PT-801` ≥ 75 PSI |\r\n| **4** | Supply: hydrant or upstream BWA tap via `PSV-100` into `TK-100` air gap **or** potable tanker | `PT-100` ≥ 20 PSI; `LIT-100` filling |\r\n| **5** | Waste to approved **downstream** sanitary sewer cleanout (bypassing grease trap!), dechlor running | `P-503` running on waste flow |\r\n| **6** | **CRITICAL:** building service valve **CLOSED** and tagged (utility/facility) | Valve shut; street meter shows 0 GPM |\r\n| **7** | Startup flush passed; DIT passed; Fill & Prove all green (`≥ 3,000 gal` or `≥ 600 gal` CT turnover) | `AIT-201` < 0.05 NTU; UV ≥ 40 mJ/cm²; CT ≥ required |\r\n| **8** | Bac-T samples pulled and logged | Chain-of-custody form complete |\r\n| **9** | Hose sanitized (50 mg/L, 30 min), flushed, connection sprayed with 200 ppm | Hose-end residual matches finished water |\r\n| **10** | Building filled at **30 PSI**, air bled, ramped to **60–70 PSI (Restaurant)** or **75–85 PSI (Hospital)** | `PT-603` steady; farthest tap Cl₂ ≥ 1.0 mg/L |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Decommissioning & Return to City Water\r\n\r\nOnce the utility restores pressure and the Boil Water Advisory is officially lifted:\r\n1. **Confirm city water:** Facility staff and the utility confirm street pressure > 50 PSI and city chlorine residual upstream of the closed street valve.\r\n2. **Isolate trailer discharge:** Close `XV-602` on the HMI and close the building's emergency inlet valve.\r\n3. **Re-open the street valve:** The utility or facility slowly opens the building's service valve. Follow health department post-advisory flushing guidelines for ice machines, beverage dispensers, and building lines.\r\n4. **Sanitize & stow hoses:** Drain potable hoses, cap both ends, and stow them on their reels. Keep raw (blue) and potable (white) hoses in separate compartments.\r\n5. **Drain ground tanks:** Drain `TK-401` and `TK-100` through the waste header with dechlorination running, then dry and fold them.\r\n6. **Execute `MODE 5: DRAIN & PRESERVE`:**\r\n   * Press **`[PREPARE FOR TRANSPORT]`** on the HMI.\r\n   * The PLC drains the clearwell, strainer, and UF feed sides, then **fills the UF trains with membrane preservative** (glycol-based in freezing weather). Membranes are never blown dry.\r\n   * When the HMI shows **`DRAINED & PRESERVED`**, disconnect the waste hose, secure chemicals within DOT materials-of-trade limits, retract the jacks, and weigh the rig on a certified scale if the load has changed.\r\n","MWTP_250_Bank_Loan_Business_Plan.md":"# MWTP-50 & MWTP-250 Commercial Loan Business Plan: Emergency Mobile Water Treatment & Critical Facility Pressurization Services\r\n\r\n**Prepared For:** Commercial Lending & SBA 7(a) / Equipment Finance Underwriting  \r\n**Asset Platforms:**\r\n* **Phase 1 / Restaurant Fleet Unit (`MWTP-50-GN22` \"KitchenShield-50\"):** 35 GPM continuous / 50 GPM peak, 22-ft Compact Gooseneck (`9,990 lb GVWR` — **No Class A CDL Required**)\r\n* **Phase 2 / Hospital Flagship Unit (`MWTP-250`):** 250 GPM rated, 36-ft Triple-Axle Gooseneck (`30,000 lb GVWR` — **Class A CDL Required**)  \r\n**Date:** November 2026 (Revision B)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Executive Summary\r\n\r\n### 1.1 Business Concept & Value Proposition\r\n**AmeriPro Environmental Services** provides rapid-response, treated, pressurized drinking water to **commercial restaurant groups, hotels, hospitals, healthcare campuses, and municipalities** during Boil Water Advisories, water outages, and planned utility shutdowns.\r\n\r\nWhen a storm, freeze, or main break compromises a city water system, critical facilities face serious disruption:\r\n* **Restaurants, hotels, and food facilities** are typically required by the health department to stop using tap water, or close, during a Boil Water Advisory unless they have an approved alternate source — losing $8,000–$25,000+ per day in gross sales and risking perishable inventory.\r\n* **Hospitals** can lose water for sterilization, dialysis, sanitation, and HVAC — which can force service cuts, diversions, or evacuations.\r\n\r\nUnlike bulk water tankers, which deliver unpressurized water into storage, both the **MWTP-50** and **MWTP-250** treat water on site through 0.02 μm ultrafiltration (integrity-tested daily), validated UV, and chlorine with measured contact time, and **pressurize the building's plumbing at 60–85 PSI**. At a pre-surveyed site with a pre-installed tie-in, building water is expected about **2 hours after arrival for a restaurant (`MWTP-50`)** or **3 hours for a hospital (`MWTP-250`)**. Supply comes from a hydrant (or upstream restaurant tap on the `MWTP-50`) that holds ≥ 20 PSI residual, a potable tanker delivery, or an approved surface source, always through an air-gap break tank.\r\n\r\n### 1.2 Phased Loan Request Options & Debt Service Highlights\r\nTo give the lender and borrower maximum flexibility, this plan underwrites **two modular equipment tiers** that can be financed sequentially (**Phase 1 `MWTP-50` first**, then **Phase 2 `MWTP-250`**) or together:\r\n\r\n| Underwriting Option | Target Market & CDL Status | Total Project Cost | Borrower Equity (15%) | SBA 7(a) Loan Request (85%) | Annual Debt Service (8.75%, 10 Yr) | Year 1 DSCR (EBITDA / After Draw) | Year 1 Retainers vs. Fixed Costs + Debt |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **Option 1A: `MWTP-50-GN22` (Using Existing Fleet 1-Ton Pickup)** | AmeriPro F.O.G. & Hood Restaurant Accounts (**No CDL**) | **$260,000** | **$39,000** | **$221,000** | **$33,238 / yr** ($2,770/mo) | **1.76×** *(1.01× after $25k draw)* | **21%** of fixed costs + debt (10-location cap) |\r\n| **Option 1B: `MWTP-50-GN22` (With New F-350 SRW Gooseneck Truck)** | AmeriPro F.O.G. & Hood Restaurant Accounts (**No CDL**) | **$325,000** | **$48,750** | **$276,250** | **$41,547 / yr** ($3,462/mo) | **1.41×** *(0.81× after $25k draw)* | **19%** of fixed costs + debt (10-location cap) |\r\n| **Option 2: `MWTP-250` Hospital Flagship (With Class 5 Truck)** | Acute-Care Hospitals & Municipalities (**Class A CDL**) | **$769,150** | **$115,370** | **$653,780** | **$98,328 / yr** ($8,194/mo) | **1.58×** *(1.03× after $55k draw)* | **32%** of fixed costs + debt |\r\n\r\n* **Why Option 1 (`MWTP-50-GN22`) Is the Lowest-Risk Entry for Lender & Borrower:**\r\n  1. **Existing Customer Base:** AmeriPro already services commercial kitchens for F.O.G. (grease traps) and exhaust hood cleaning. Bundling a $150/month single-store ($1,800/yr) or $125/month-per-store group ($1,500/yr per location) standby membership into existing accounts requires little cold-calling.\r\n  2. **Low Break-Even:** About **seven 3-day restaurant deployments a year** cover all fixed costs and the loan payment on Option 1A even with no retainers (§5.3). Retainers — capped at 10 covered locations per unit so every customer's call order is honest — add steady cash on top.\r\n  3. **No Class A CDL Bottleneck:** At `9,990 lb GVWR` behind a 1-ton pickup (`GCWR < 26,001 lbs`), any AmeriPro technician with a standard license and DOT medical card can tow it.\r\n  4. **Regulatory Track Record:** Establishes AmeriPro's Georgia EPD emergency-use approval and field logs on low-complexity commercial kitchens before or alongside launching the $769k `MWTP-250` hospital flagship.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Use of Loan Proceeds & Collateral (Option 1 vs. Option 2)\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Option 1 — `MWTP-50-GN22` (\"KitchenShield-50\") Compact Restaurant Unit\r\n| Asset / Expenditure Category | Option 1A (Fleet Truck) | Option 1B (New Truck) | Borrower Equity (15%) | Bank Loan (85%) | Collateral & Lien Status |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| **MWTP-50-GN22 Compact Gooseneck Trailer** (22-ft, 9,990 lb GVWR, 20 kW diesel gen, 50A shore ATS, 4-module UF skid, 2 UV reactors, duplex booster, PLC; incl. PE engineering & 15% contingency) | $238,625 | $238,625 | $35,794 | $202,831 | **1st Priority UCC-1 & Vehicle Title Lien** |\r\n| **Tow Vehicle: 1-Ton SRW Pickup (F-350 / Ram 3500 SRW w/ Gooseneck Ball)** | $0 *(Existing)* | $62,000 | $0 / $9,300 | $0 / $52,700 | **1st Priority Vehicle Title Lien** |\r\n| **Initial Inventory, Spare Membranes, Adapter Tees, Hose Kit & Field Lab** | $6,375 | $7,375 | $956 / $1,106 | $5,419 / $6,269 | Blanket UCC-1 Business Assets |\r\n| **Insurance, State Plan Review, DOT Med Card Setup & Working Capital Reserve** | $15,000 | $17,000 | $2,250 / $2,550 | $12,750 / $14,450 | Operating Reserve Account |\r\n| **TOTAL OPTION 1 PROJECT FINANCING** | **$260,000** | **$325,000** | **$39,000 / $48,750** | **$221,000 / $276,250** | **Loan-to-Cost: 85%** |\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Option 2 — `MWTP-250` Hospital Flagship Unit (36-ft Gooseneck)\r\n| Asset / Expenditure Category | Total Cost | Borrower Equity (15%) | Bank Loan Proceeds (85%) | Collateral & Lien Status |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| **MWTP-250 Mobile Water Treatment Trailer** (titled gooseneck, 80 kW generator, 14-module UF skid, 3 UV reactors, triplex booster, Rockwell PLC; incl. engineering & contingency) | $601,450 | $90,218 | $511,232 | **1st Priority UCC-1 & Vehicle Title Lien** |\r\n| **Tow Vehicle: Class 5 Chassis Cab (F-550 / Ram 5500 class) with Gooseneck Flatbed** | $95,000 | $14,250 | $80,750 | **1st Priority Vehicle Title Lien** |\r\n| **Initial Chemical Inventory, Spare Membranes, Hose Kits & Field Lab** | $12,700 | $1,905 | $10,795 | Blanket UCC-1 Business Assets |\r\n| **Insurance, State Approvals, DOT/CDL Compliance Setup, Legal & Working Capital / Standby Payroll Reserve** | $60,000 | $8,997 | $51,003 | Operating Reserve Account |\r\n| **TOTAL OPTION 2 PROJECT FINANCING** | **$769,150** | **$115,370** | **$653,780** | **Loan-to-Cost: 85%** |\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> **Collateral, stated plainly:** Both trailers are custom integrations. Lenders typically value specialized equipment at orderly liquidation value, which may be below build cost. The strongest collateral pieces are the titled truck and trailer chassis, the diesel generator, and standard 316SS pumps and skids, which have secondary markets. Obtain an independent equipment appraisal if the lender requests one.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Market Analysis & Regulatory Demand Drivers\r\n\r\n### 3.1 Why Commercial Kitchens & Hospitals Pay for This Service\r\n1. **Commercial Kitchens & AmeriPro Account Synergy (`MWTP-50` Primary Market):**\r\n   * Under state and county food codes (FDA Food Code adoption), a restaurant under a Boil Water Advisory must stop using tap water for food prep, ice, beverages, and warewashing unless it has an **approved alternate water source**. Most restaurants stay open on workarounds — bottled water, bagged ice, disposable service ware, and a limited menu — rather than closing.\r\n   * The `MWTP-50` is worth the most where those workarounds break down: **high-volume full-service restaurants and hotel kitchens**, **multi-unit chains** with brand standards, **institutional kitchens** (nursing homes, assisted living, schools, correctional facilities), and **food processors and commissaries**. For these customers, a 3-day deployment ($2,500 mobilization + $3,200/day) costs less than the lost margin, workaround supplies, and spoiled inventory of running degraded or closing.\r\n   * Because AmeriPro already visits commercial kitchens on recurring schedules for **F.O.G. (grease trap pumping)** and **exhaust hood cleaning**, AmeriPro has warm relationships with restaurant owners and direct access to map their water shutoffs and downstream sanitary cleanouts at virtually zero customer-acquisition cost.\r\n2. **Hospital Emergency Preparedness Requirements (`MWTP-250` Primary Market):**\r\n   * The **CMS Emergency Preparedness Rule (42 CFR § 482.15)** requires hospitals to have an emergency plan, including policies for subsistence needs such as water and for alternate sources of energy and utilities.\r\n   * **Joint Commission** emergency management standards expect hospitals to plan how they would manage utilities, including water, for up to **96 hours** — whether by staying open, scaling back, or evacuating. This is a planning expectation, not a mandate to buy pressurization.\r\n   * A **standby agreement with a mobile treatment provider**, documented in the hospital's Emergency Water Supply Plan with a pre-installed connection and an annual drill, is a practical way to support that plan without building a permanent backup plant.\r\n3. **Aging Infrastructure and Boil Water Advisories:**\r\n   * The U.S. sees roughly **240,000 water main breaks per year** (ASCE estimate) plus storms, freezes, and power-related pressure losses that trigger Boil Water Advisories.\r\n4. **Insurance & Disaster Reimbursement:**\r\n   * Some customers **may** recover costs under business-interruption, utility-service-interruption, or off-premises-power endorsements, which often have exclusions and waiting periods. Public and some private nonprofit facilities **may** qualify for FEMA Public Assistance after a declared disaster. Customers should confirm with their insurer — we won't promise reimbursement.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Revenue Model & Pricing Schedule (`MWTP-50` vs. `MWTP-250`)\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart LR\r\n    subgraph M50[\"Option 1: MWTP-50 Compact Restaurant Unit\"]\r\n        K_RET[\"KitchenShield Retainers ($1,800/yr single or $1,500/yr per group store, max 10 per unit)\"]\r\n        K_DEP[\"Restaurant Dispatches ($2,500 Mob + $3,200/day)\"]\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    subgraph M250[\"Option 2: MWTP-250 Hospital Flagship Unit\"]\r\n        H_RET[\"Hospital Priority Retainers ($18,000/yr each, max 3 per trailer)\"]\r\n        H_DEP[\"Hospital / Municipal Dispatches ($6,500 Mob + $7,500/day)\"]\r\n        PLANNED[\"Planned Utility Bypass ($4,200/day, 3-day min)\"]\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    M50 & M250 --> CF[\"Year-Round Recurring + Event Cash Flow\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 4.1 Capacity Discipline\r\nOne storm or main break can affect multiple customers at once, and each trailer can serve **one** connection at a time:\r\n* **`MWTP-50` Restaurant Unit:** Dedicated strictly to commercial kitchens and hospitality accounts (never pulled off a restaurant job for a hospital). Each unit carries **no more than 10 covered locations per region**, ranked in a call order written into every agreement; group stores count toward the cap. One kitchen is pressurized at a time, and coverage grows by adding units rather than selling more retainers on the same trailer.\r\n* **`MWTP-250` Hospital Unit:** **No more than 3 hospital priority retainers per trailer per region**, ranked first-call / second-call / third-call in the contract, with that ranking disclosed to each hospital. Commercial retainers on the `MWTP-250` are \"served as available\" after hospital commitments.\r\n\r\n### 4.2 Pricing Schedule & Unit Economics Comparison\r\n\r\n| Revenue Stream | Unit | Target Client | Pricing Structure | Direct Variable Cost | Gross Margin |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: |\r\n| **1A. KitchenShield Single-Store Retainer** | `MWTP-50` | High-volume restaurants & institutional kitchens | **$1,800 / yr** ($150/mo bundled w/ F.O.G./hood invoice) | ~$100/yr (survey check) | ~94% |\r\n| **1B. KitchenShield Group Retainer (2+ Stores)** | `MWTP-50` | Multi-unit restaurant groups & hotels | **$1,500 / yr per store** ($125/mo per store; counts toward 10-location cap) | ~$100/yr per store | ~93% |\r\n| **2A. Restaurant Emergency Mobilization** | `MWTP-50` | Restaurant & kitchen dispatches | **$2,500 / event** — tow, ground tanks, DIT, hose sanitization | ~$450 / event | ~82% |\r\n| **2B. Restaurant 24-Hr Treatment Day** | `MWTP-50` | Commercial kitchens (35–50 GPM) | **$3,200 / day** | **$669/day** (shore) to **$749/day** (diesel gen) | **76.6%–79.1%** |\r\n| **3A. Hospital Priority Retainer (Max 3)** | `MWTP-250` | Regional hospitals (ranked 1st/2nd/3rd) | **$18,000 / yr** — site survey, tie-in design, annual drill | ~$1,200/yr | ~93% |\r\n| **3B. Hospital / Municipal Mobilization** | `MWTP-250` | Hospital & municipal dispatches | **$6,500 / event** | ~$1,200 / event | ~82% |\r\n| **3C. Hospital 24-Hr Treatment Day** | `MWTP-250` | Hospitals, municipalities (250 GPM) | **$7,500 / day** | **~$1,911 / day** (2 operators + fuel/chems) | ~75% |\r\n| **3D. Planned Non-Emergency Rental** | `MWTP-250` | Utilities (main rehab), events | **$4,200 / day** (3-day min) | **~$1,661 / day** | ~60% |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Option 1 Financial Projections: `MWTP-50-GN22` Compact Restaurant Unit (Years 1–3)\r\n\r\n### 5.1 Utilization & Retainer Assumptions (`MWTP-50-GN22`)\r\n* **Year 1:** **8 covered locations** ($12,900 = 3 single stores @ $1,800 + 5 group stores @ $1,500, from AmeriPro's existing F.O.G./hood accounts) + **6 emergency events / 25 active treatment days** (~7% utilization). Deployments include non-retainer customers served at the same rates when the unit is free.\r\n* **Year 2:** **10 covered locations — the per-unit cap** ($16,200 = 4 single + 6 group stores) + **10 emergency events / 45 active treatment days** (~12% utilization).\r\n* **Year 3:** **10 covered locations** ($16,200) + **14 emergency events / 60 active treatment days** (~16% utilization). Demand beyond the cap is the trigger for a second unit.\r\n* **Staffing:** a certified operator attends through the restaurant's service hours (~12 hours); overnight the unit runs on cellular telemetry with automatic outlet lockout and a one-hour on-call response, subject to the state's emergency-use approval.\r\n\r\n### 5.2 `MWTP-50-GN22` Income Statement & Debt Service Coverage (Years 1–3)\r\n\r\n| Financial Line Item (`MWTP-50-GN22` Single Unit) | Year 1 (25 Active Days) | Year 2 (45 Active Days) | Year 3 (60 Active Days) |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **REVENUE** | | | |\r\n| KitchenShield Standby Retainers (Bundled w/ F.O.G. & Hood Accounts) | $12,900 *(8 locations)* | $16,200 *(10 — cap)* | $16,200 *(10 — cap)* |\r\n| Emergency Mobilization & Sanitization Fees ($2,500/event) | $15,000 *(6 events)* | $25,000 *(10 events)* | $35,000 *(14 events)* |\r\n| Active Restaurant Treatment Days ($3,200/day) | $80,000 *(25 days)* | $144,000 *(45 days)* | $192,000 *(60 days)* |\r\n| **TOTAL GROSS REVENUE** | **$107,900** | **$185,200** | **$243,200** |\r\n| **DIRECT COST OF GOODS SOLD (COGS)** | | | |\r\n| Diesel / Shore Power, Chemicals, Bags & Bac-T Lab ($169/day avg) | $4,225 | $7,605 | $10,140 |\r\n| Attended Operator Shift + Overnight On-Call/Telemetry ($580/active day) | $14,500 | $26,100 | $34,800 |\r\n| Truck Fuel & Mobilization Consumables ($450/event) | $2,700 | $4,500 | $6,300 |\r\n| **TOTAL DIRECT COGS** | **$21,425** | **$38,205** | **$51,240** |\r\n| **GROSS PROFIT** | **$86,475 (80.1%)** | **$146,995 (79.4%)** | **$191,960 (78.9%)** |\r\n| **FIXED OPERATING EXPENSES (OPEX)** | | | |\r\n| Commercial Auto, GL, Pollution & Inland Marine Insurance *(Non-CDL tier)* | $14,500 | $15,200 | $16,000 |\r\n| Preventive Maintenance, CIP Chemicals & Analyzer Calibration | $4,200 | $4,500 | $4,800 |\r\n| UF Membrane (4 modules) & UV Lamp Replacement Reserve | $3,200 | $3,200 | $3,200 |\r\n| Operator On-Call Stipend, Licensing, Telemetry & Accounting | $6,100 | $6,600 | $7,000 |\r\n| **TOTAL FIXED OPEX** | **$28,000** | **$29,500** | **$31,000** |\r\n| **EBITDA (Cash Flow Available for Debt Service)** | **$58,475** | **$117,495** | **$160,960** |\r\n| Less: Division Manager Allocation / Owner Draw | ($25,000) | ($35,000) | ($45,000) |\r\n| **NET CASH AVAILABLE FOR DEBT SERVICE (After Draw)** | **$33,475** | **$82,495** | **$115,960** |\r\n| **Option 1A Debt Service ($221,000 Loan — Existing Fleet Truck)** | **$33,238** | **$33,238** | **$33,238** |\r\n| **Option 1B Debt Service ($276,250 Loan — With New 1-Ton Truck)** | **$41,547** | **$41,547** | **$41,547** |\r\n| **OPTION 1A DSCR (Existing Truck: EBITDA / After Draw)** | **1.76×** *(1.01×)* | **3.53×** *(2.48×)* | **4.84×** *(3.49×)* |\r\n| **OPTION 1B DSCR (With New Truck: EBITDA / After Draw)** | **1.41×** *(0.81×)* | **2.83×** *(1.99×)* | **3.87×** *(2.79×)* |\r\n\r\n**Year 1 note:** after the owner draw, Year 1 coverage is thin (1.01× on Option 1A, 0.81× on Option 1B). Use the existing fleet truck (Option 1A), defer the Year 1 draw, or request interest-only payments during the build to keep Year 1 above the lender's minimum.\r\n\r\n### 5.3 `MWTP-50-GN22` Break-Even & Downside Stress Tests\r\n* **Annual fixed costs + debt service (Year 1):**\r\n  * **Option 1A (Existing Truck):** $28,000 Fixed OpEx + $33,238 Debt = **$61,238 / year**.\r\n  * **Option 1B (With New Truck):** $28,000 Fixed OpEx + $41,547 Debt = **$69,547 / year**.\r\n* **Each 3-day restaurant deployment nets $9,403** ($2,500 mob + 3 × $3,200 − $450 mob cost − 3 × $749 daily cost).\r\n* **Stress Test 1 — Year 1 Retainers, Few Deployments:** Year 1 retainers ($12,900) cover **46% of fixed OpEx**. The rest of fixed costs plus debt takes **six 3-day deployments (18 active days)** on Option 1A or **seven (21 days)** on Option 1B.\r\n* **Stress Test 2 — Zero Retainers (Deployments Only):** With no retainers, **seven 3-day deployments (21 active days)** cover all fixed OpEx and debt service on Option 1A ($65,821 vs. $61,238), and **eight (24 days)** on Option 1B ($75,224 vs. $69,547).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Option 2 Financial Projections: `MWTP-250` Hospital Flagship Unit (Years 1–3)\r\n\r\n### 6.1 Utilization Assumptions (`MWTP-250`)\r\n* **Year 1:** 2 hospital retainers, 8 commercial retainers, **3 emergency events / 25 treatment days**, **15 planned rental days** (2 jobs). 40 active days ≈ 11% utilization.\r\n* **Year 2:** 3 hospital retainers, 12 commercial, **5 events / 40 days**, **25 planned days** (3 jobs). ≈ 18% utilization.\r\n* **Year 3:** 3 hospital retainers (cap), 15 commercial, **6 events / 50 days**, **30 planned days** (4 jobs). ≈ 22% utilization.\r\n\r\n### 6.2 `MWTP-250` Income Statement & Cash Flow Coverage (Years 1–3)\r\n\r\n| Financial Line Item (`MWTP-250` Single Unit) | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **REVENUE** | | | |\r\n| Hospital Priority Retainers ($18,000/yr) | $36,000 *(2)* | $54,000 *(3)* | $54,000 *(3)* |\r\n| Commercial Standby Retainers ($3,500/yr) | $28,000 *(8)* | $42,000 *(12)* | $52,500 *(15)* |\r\n| Emergency Mobilization Fees ($6,500/event) | $19,500 *(3)* | $32,500 *(5)* | $39,000 *(6)* |\r\n| Emergency Treatment Days ($7,500/day) | $187,500 *(25 d)* | $300,000 *(40 d)* | $375,000 *(50 d)* |\r\n| Planned Rental Days ($4,200/day) | $63,000 *(15 d)* | $105,000 *(25 d)* | $126,000 *(30 d)* |\r\n| **TOTAL GROSS REVENUE** | **$334,000** | **$533,500** | **$646,500** |\r\n| **DIRECT COST OF GOODS SOLD (COGS)** | | | |\r\n| Fuel, Chemicals, Bags & Bac-T Lab ($451/active day) | $18,040 | $29,315 | $36,080 |\r\n| Two-Operator Crew + Per Diem ($1,460/emergency day, $1,210/planned day) | $54,650 | $88,650 | $109,300 |\r\n| Truck Fuel & Mobilization ($1,200/emergency event, $800/planned job) | $5,200 | $8,400 | $10,400 |\r\n| **TOTAL COGS** | **$77,890** | **$126,365** | **$155,780** |\r\n| **GROSS PROFIT** | **$256,110 (76.7%)** | **$407,135 (76.3%)** | **$490,720 (75.9%)** |\r\n| **FIXED OPERATING EXPENSES (OPEX)** | | | |\r\n| Auto, General Liability, Pollution & Inland Marine Insurance | $38,000 | $39,500 | $41,000 |\r\n| Equipment Storage Yard / Heated Bay Lease | $9,600 | $9,900 | $10,200 |\r\n| Preventive Maintenance, CIP & Calibration | $12,000 | $12,500 | $13,000 |\r\n| Membrane & UV Replacement Reserve (5–7 yr life) | $9,000 | $9,000 | $9,000 |\r\n| Sales, Hospital Marketing, SAM.gov / FEMA Vendor Registration | $8,500 | $9,000 | $9,500 |\r\n| Accounting, Legal, Licensing & Telemetry | $5,200 | $5,500 | $5,800 |\r\n| On-Call Standby Pay (2 certified operators / CDL drivers) | $12,000 | $12,500 | $13,000 |\r\n| DOT Compliance (drug & alcohol consortium, medical cards, annual inspection, permits) | $3,500 | $3,600 | $3,700 |\r\n| Operator Certification & Training | $2,500 | $2,600 | $2,700 |\r\n| **TOTAL FIXED OPEX** | **$100,300** | **$104,100** | **$107,900** |\r\n| **EBITDA (Cash Flow Available for Debt Service)** | **$155,810** | **$303,035** | **$382,820** |\r\n| Less: Owner Base Salary / Draw | ($55,000) | ($65,000) | ($75,000) |\r\n| **NET CASH AVAILABLE FOR BANK DEBT SERVICE** | **$100,810** | **$238,035** | **$307,820** |\r\n| **Annual Bank Loan Payment ($653,780 @ 8.75%, 10 Yrs)** | **$98,328** | **$98,328** | **$98,328** |\r\n| **PRE-TAX FREE CASH SURPLUS (After Debt & Salary)** | **$2,482** | **$139,707** | **$209,492** |\r\n| **DSCR (EBITDA Basis)** | **1.58×** *(after draw: 1.03×)* | **3.08×** *(after draw: 2.42×)* | **3.89×** *(after draw: 3.13×)* |\r\n\r\n### 6.3 `MWTP-250` Break-Even & Downside Stress Tests\r\n* **Annual fixed costs + debt service (Year 1):** $100,300 + $98,328 = **$198,628**.\r\n* **Case A — Retainers only (zero deployments):** A full book of 3 hospital + 15 commercial retainers brings in **$106,500 (54% of fixed costs + debt)**. Unlike the `MWTP-50`, retainers alone on the `MWTP-250` **do not** carry the loan; deployments are required.\r\n* **Case B — Deployments only (zero retainers):** Each emergency treatment day contributes **$5,589** ($7,500 − $1,911). The trailer needs about **36 emergency treatment days per year (~10% utilization)** to cover fixed costs and debt, before mobilization fees.\r\n* **Year 1 mitigation (if building `MWTP-250` standalone):** Request interest-only payments during the ~6-month build, raise the equity injection, defer the owner draw until the second hospital retainer is signed — **or launch Phase 1 (`MWTP-50-GN22`) first to generate immediate positive cash flow**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Risk Management & Regulatory Compliance\r\n\r\n| Identified Risk | Engineering & Operational Mitigation |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Water Quality Liability** | Credit-based barriers on both units: UF with NSF 419 challenge-tested modules and a **daily pressure-decay integrity test (`DIT-201`)**; EPA UVDGM-validated UV (`1+1` on `MWTP-50`, `2+1` on `MWTP-250`) with UVT monitoring; **chlorine contact time calculated live** (`600-gal` baffled tank on `MWTP-50`, `3,000-gal` on `MWTP-250`) for 4-log virus inactivation. Any trip closes `XV-602`. Bac-T samples to a state-certified lab per the state's emergency-use approval. |\r\n| **Drawing from a Compromised Main** | Neither trailer ever pumps directly from the main. Hydrant or upstream BWA tap supply runs through a **pressure-sustaining valve (`PSV-100`) that holds ≥ 20 PSI residual** and an **air-gap break tank (`TK-100`)**. If residual can't be held, supply switches to potable tanker drops (only 1 drop/day needed for a restaurant on the `MWTP-50`). |\r\n| **Regulatory Approval** | Before launch: PE-stamped basis of design; pre-application meeting and plan review with the state drinking water program (**Georgia EPD Drinking Water Program** in our home state); state-certified water treatment operators on every crew. We will not claim \"pre-registration\" until a state has actually issued an approval. |\r\n| **Overselling One Asset** | Hospital retainers capped at 3 per `MWTP-250` trailer per region with ranked priority disclosed in each contract (§4.1). Deploying the dedicated `MWTP-50` for restaurants eliminates any conflict between hospital and restaurant dispatches. |\r\n| **Commercial Driver Licensing** | **`MWTP-50-GN22`:** `9,990 lb GVWR` gooseneck behind a 1-ton pickup (`GCWR < 26,001 lbs`) → **No Class A CDL required** (standard license + DOT medical card). **`MWTP-250`:** `GCWR ≈ 49,500 lb` → **Class A CDL required** (minimum 2 CDL drivers budgeted). |\r\n| **Chemical Safety & Hauling** | Segregated oxidizer and acid cabinets, chlorine gas detector (`AIT-901`), ventilated chemical bay, ANSI Z358.1 eyewash. Chemicals hauled within DOT materials-of-trade limits; bulk bleach bought fresh near the job. |\r\n| **Waste Water & F.O.G. Protection** | All waste is dechlorinated (`P-503`) and discharged to the **downstream sanitary sewer cleanout** with utility/facility permission — never through a restaurant's F.O.G. grease interceptor and never to storm drains without MS4 permission. |\r\n| **Single Points of Failure** | Standby UV reactor, duplex/triplex booster pumps, duplex air compressors, duplex chlorine pumps. On the `MWTP-50`, **dual power capability** allows running on a restaurant's **240V 50A shore receptacle** or the onboard **20 kW diesel generator**. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 8. Management & Execution Roadmap\r\n\r\n### 8.1 Management Team\r\n* **Jeff Ricks, Technical Director** — engineering, operations, and the company safety program.\r\n* **State-certified water treatment operators** — on every deployment.\r\n* **Class A CDL drivers** — for the MWTP-250 (Phase 2).\r\n* **Consulting PE** — licensed water-treatment engineer for the basis of design and state submittals.\r\n\r\nPersonal and corporate financial statements and resumes accompany the loan application.\r\n\r\n### 8.2 Phased Launch Timeline\r\n* **Phase 1 — `MWTP-50-GN22` Restaurant Unit (Months 0–6):**\r\n  1. **Months 0–2:** PE basis of design for `MWTP-50-GN22`; Georgia EPD pre-application meeting; vendor quotes; sign standby LOIs for 8–10 covered locations from AmeriPro's existing F.O.G. and hood accounts; close Option 1 SBA loan ($221k–$276k).\r\n  2. **Months 2–5:** Fabricate 22-ft compact gooseneck, integrate 4-module UF, UV, and booster skids; perform Factory Acceptance Test (FAT) and Georgia EPD inspection.\r\n  3. **Month 6:** Complete restaurant site surveys (mapping shutoffs, adapter tees, downstream cleanouts, and 50A shore plugs); activate monthly retainer billing (~$13,000–$16,000/yr run rate) and 24/7 dispatch readiness.\r\n* **Phase 2 — `MWTP-250` Hospital Flagship Unit (Months 6–15):**\r\n  1. Leverage Phase 1 state approval, operator team, and cash flow to secure 2–3 Hospital Priority LOIs ($18,000/yr each) and close Option 2 financing ($653.8k loan) for the 36-ft `MWTP-250`.\r\n"};
